  {
    "exam_code": "sk0-005",
    "topic": 1,
    "question_num": 27,
    "question": "A server administrator needs to harden a server by only allowing secure traffic and DNS inquiries. A port scan reports the following ports are open:\n443\n636\nWhich of the following open ports should be closed to secure the server properly? (Choose two.)",
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "21",
        "is_correct": true
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "22",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "23",
        "is_correct": true
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "53",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "E",
        "text": "443",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "F",
        "text": "636",
        "is_correct": false
      }
    ],
    "suggested_answers": [
      "A",
      "C"
    ],
    "answers": [
      "21 - FTP\n22 - SSH\n23 - Telnet\n53 - DNS\n443 - HTTPS\n636 - LDAPS\n\nThere are 3 ports which not secure and need to close 21, 23, 53 but the server provide DNS inquiries, so we don't close port 53.",
      "DNS can also use other ports for specialized purposes. For example, DNS over TLS (DoT) uses port 853, and DNS over HTTPS (DoH) typically uses port 443 to encapsulate DNS traffic within HTTPS\n\nThat's why 53 is blocked",
      "They shouldn't have ftp in the answer results. But since they said securing traffic and DNS I think the puposed answer of CD and is what they're looking for, they're just made it a trick question to try to glean more exam fees from people for a cert no one respects.",
      "Then get a new scanner because it is not showing Ports 21 and 23",
      "How could DNS Port 53 needs to be close where the question asking for DNS Port to be used ?",
      "A - Why would you leave an unsecure FTP port open? C - Telnet is notorious for sending credentials over the network in plain text. If you close port 53 how will hosts on the network get name resolution?",
      "No ftp or telnet allowed",
      "A and C should be the best answer",
      "AC\nPort 21 and File Transfer\nFTP is often thought of as a “not secure” file transfer protocol. This is mainly due to FTP sending data in clear text and offering an anonymous option with no password required. However, FTP is a trusted and still widely used protocol for transferring files\n\nIs port 23 secure?\nPort 23 – Telnet. A predecessor to SSH, is no longer considered secure and is frequently abused by malware.",
      "53 is for DNS query, why we need to close it?"
    ],
    "url": "https://www.examtopics.com/discussions/comptia/view/82213-exam-sk0-005-topic-1-question-27-discussion/"
  },
