  {
    "exam_code": "sk0-005",
    "topic": 1,
    "question_num": 29,
    "question": "Which of the following must a server administrator do to ensure data on the SAN is not compromised if it is leaked?",
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "Encrypt the data that is leaving the SAN",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "Encrypt the data at rest",
        "is_correct": true
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "Encrypt the host servers",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "Encrypt all the network traffic",
        "is_correct": false
      }
    ],
    "suggested_answers": [
      "B"
    ],
    "answers": [
      "Encrypting data at rest ensures that the data stored on the SAN is protected from unauthorized access, even if the storage medium is compromised or stolen. This is a critical step to secure sensitive information and mitigate the risk of data breaches.",
      "B. Encrypting Data at rest would ensure the data is unreadable if it is leaked.",
      "To ensure data on the SAN (Storage Area Network) is not compromised if it is leaked, the server administrator should:\n\nB. Encrypt the data at rest\n\nEncrypting the data at rest ensures that even if the data is accessed or leaked, it remains unreadable without the appropriate decryption key. This provides a layer of security against unauthorized access to the data stored on the SAN.\n~ChatGpT4",
      "B. To ensure data on the SAN is not compromised if it is leaked, a server administrator should encrypt the data at rest. This means that the data is encrypted while it is stored on the SAN. This way, even if the data is accessed or copied without authorization, it will be unreadable without the encryption key.\n\nEncrypting the data that is leaving the SAN or encrypting all network traffic can help protect data while it is in transit, but it does not necessarily protect the data if it is leaked from the SAN. Encrypting the host servers may also be a good security practice, but it does not directly address the issue of data leakage from the SAN.",
      "It's referring to data ON the SAN.\nData at rest.",
      "I concur with answer B. I see answer D achieving the need for SAN traffic being encrypted. Why have A and D as D would take care of said traffic? The encryption of data at rest is what we are looking for.",
      "Coming back to this a little later.. Now I am thinking answer A is in-fact correct.",
      "I can accept B but I wish they had not included D as an option. Basic cyber-security principle: protection of data in motion, at rest, in process.",
      "Encrypt Data at Rest on the Storage Array of drives.",
      "Answer is B. Because the question states \"data on the SAN\" so if you encrypt data at rest, this will prevent data compromised.",
      "Best Guess is A, Encrypt all data leaving the SAN. Data can leave through local transfer (USB drive) a network vulnerability from outsider/insider, . So data needs to be encrypted at rest, but also possibly in transit. But if you can encrypt \"all data that leaves SAN\" then that would cover everything... all these questions seem very ambiguous......",
      "SAN is a network, the whole idea of this approach is for multiple devices to be able to access a shared filesystem, not DAS,, which is the Storage device attached to a server that SAN can use to share a filesystem across multiple devices on the network\n\nData Loss(breach) Prevention Method:\n1; Encrypt all \"SAN\" network traffic..\n2; Another approach is to Encrypt Data at Rest on the Storage Array of drives.\n\nThey both go together like peanut butter and jelly for a perfect sandwich (Data Breach Protection on SAN)."
    ],
    "url": "https://www.examtopics.com/discussions/comptia/view/78022-exam-sk0-005-topic-1-question-29-discussion/"
  },
  {
    "exam_code": "sk0-005",
    "topic": 1,
    "question_num": 30,
    "question": "A server technician has been asked to upload a few files from the internal web server to the internal FTP server. The technician logs in to the web server using\nPuTTY, but the connection to the FTP server fails. However, the FTP connection from the technician's workstation is suׁcessful. To troubleshoot the issue, the technician executes the following command on both the web server and the workstation: ping ftp.acme.local\nThe IP address in the command output is different on each machine. Which of the following is the MOST likely reason for the connection failure?",
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "A misconfigured firewall",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "A misconfigured hosts.deny file",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "A misconfigured hosts file",
        "is_correct": true
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "A misconfigured hosts.allow file",
        "is_correct": false
      }
    ],
    "suggested_answers": [
      "C"
    ],
    "answers": [
      "Hosts.Allow has nothing to do with \"ping\" command. The host file is a \"cruddy DNS\" on a local machine (both Windows and Linux). Different Host files = different DNS lookup results.",
      "C is correct",
      "C is the answer",
      "C is the answer",
      "Remove PuTTY from the question it's intended as a distraction.\nThe key is \"ping ftp.acme.local\nThe IP address in the command output is different on each machine.\"\nhosts.allow and hosts.deny are for ACL not direction\na host file that is different on each machine would cause the IP address to be different",
      "C is the answer",
      "C might be the answer. No reference of Linux, and Putty also can be used for Windows & Linux.",
      "No, the answer is D. hosts file is used in Windows. host.allow is used in Linux.\nif you're connecting the server using Putty that means the server is Linux.",
      "Windows 10 Host File is found at c:\\Windows\\System32\\Drivers\\etc\\hosts\nLinux host file is found at /etc/hosts..\n\nC: Misconfigured host file",
      "Wrong on the Putty / linux statement. It isn't used to connect ssh only to a linux box. It can be used ssh to a windows server as well."
    ],
    "url": "https://www.examtopics.com/discussions/comptia/view/73215-exam-sk0-005-topic-1-question-30-discussion/"
  },
  {
    "exam_code": "sk0-005",
    "topic": 1,
    "question_num": 31,
    "question": "A company deploys antivirus, anti-malware, and firewalls that can be assumed to be functioning properly. Which of the following is the MOST likely system vulnerability?",
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "Insider threat",
        "is_correct": true
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "Worms",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "Ransomware",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "Open ports",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "E",
        "text": "Two-person integrity",
        "is_correct": false
      }
    ],
    "suggested_answers": [
      "A"
    ],
    "answers": [
      "Users (insider threat) are the biggest problem on any properly configured network.",
      "Correct answer: A\nThe company implemented technical controls (antivirus, Anti-malware, and firewalls) and these controls are working properly.\n\nThe remaining vulnerability would be insider threat. Internal user threats (careless insider)\nUsers who unknowingly expose the system to outside threats. This is the most common type of insider threat, resulting from mistakes, such as leaving a device exposed or falling victim to a scam. For example, an employee who intends no harm may click on an insecure link, infecting the system with malware.\n\nThe biggest security vulnerability in any organization is its own employees. Whether it’s the result of intentional malfeasance or an accident, most data breaches can be traced back to a person within the organization that was breached.\nFor example, employees may abuse their access privileges for personal gain. Or, an employee may click on the wrong link in an email, download the wrong file from an online site, or give the wrong person their user account credentials—allowing attackers easy access to your systems.",
      "The rest of the options besides Insider Threats are covered by the \"...can be assumed to be functioning properly.\"",
      "keywords are important in CompTIA exams, They are identified by the capitalization of the keyword, in this case, it is MOST likely system vulnerabilities.\n\nInside Threats seem like the MOST likely system vulnerabilities, Cause Open ports vulnerabilities have been partly mitigated using the anti-malware program, the other possible attack using open ports is social engineering attacks which can basically be mitigated using awareness programs for the users.\n\nThere is no inside threat attack mitigation process outlined in the question, so I think inside threat is the MOST likely system vulnerability from the options outlined.",
      "The wording on this question leaves a lot open for assumption. Just becuase you have a Firewall doesn't mean you don't have open ports",
      "It does leave it open for assumption however, with \"...assumed to be functioning properly.\"\nI also believe them to be implying configured correctly with bare minimum needed for business operations."
    ],
    "url": "https://www.examtopics.com/discussions/comptia/view/73181-exam-sk0-005-topic-1-question-31-discussion/"
  },
  {
    "exam_code": "sk0-005",
    "topic": 1,
    "question_num": 32,
    "question": "A security analyst suspects a remote server is running vulnerable network applications. The analyst does not have administrative credentials for the server. Which of the following would MOST likely help the analyst determine if the applications are running?",
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "User account control",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "Anti-malware",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "A sniffer",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "A port scanner",
        "is_correct": true
      }
    ],
    "suggested_answers": [
      "D"
    ],
    "answers": [
      "D is the correct answer",
      "D. Port Scanner. You can run nmap to determine what ports are open and what services are running on them.",
      "User Account Control (UAC)\nAdministrator and root accounts are the most highly privileged accounts in an operating system. When a server is left logged on with a privileged account, it creates a huge security issue. Most of the server operating systems you will encounter today incorporate the ability of an administrator or a root account holder to use a nonprivileged account as standard operating procedure and elevate their privileges as needed without logging off and logging back in as root.\n\nThe User Account Control feature in Windows and the use of the sudo command in Linux make this possible. Using either system an administrator can elevate their privileges for a specific task and that security context ends when they are finished with that task.\n\nIn Windows this can be done in the GUI by right-clicking the icon representing the task and selecting Run As Administrator\n\nUAC REQUIRES administrator-level access, provided answer is not correct.",
      "Internet Control Message Protocol (ICMP) messages can be used to scan a network for open ports. Open ports indicate services that may be running and listening on a device that may be susceptible to attack. An ICMP, or port scanning, attack basically pings every address and port number combination and keeps track of which ports are open on each device as the pings are answered by open ports with listening services and not answered by closed ports. One of the most widely used port scanners is Network Mapper (Nmap), a free and open source utility for network discovery and security auditing.",
      "Never seen UAC as a way to detect the status of any network application.",
      "Provided answer may be correct. If the analyst does not have administravtive credentials for the server, will he/she be able to run the port scan on the Server/Network without admin credentials?\n\nYou can look at the UAC to see if applications are running.\n\nReference:\nhttps://docs.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/security/identity-protection/user-account-control/how-user-account-control-works",
      "@Dion... close, but not correct... you were on the right track. UAC or user account control is basically Mandatory Access Control. which REQUIRES administrator level access. That would be the correct answer IF \"analyst does not have administrative credentials\" was not in there. So the analyst needs to verify/check vulnerable applications without administrative access. welcome to the world of hackers... you run nmap or something of the sort. sniffing would work, but if traffic is encrypted, it might be more challenging. best answer is to do a port scan. D is my answer",
      "Fairly certain a port scanner would be used here. You can run nmap to determine what ports are open and what services are running on them. could even run a -sV to determine version",
      "UAC would only prevent installs. As stated in the reference link. User Account Control: Detect application installations and prompt for elevation. Does not check if an application is already installed.",
      "'MOST likely help the analyst determine if the applications are running?' not being run. Answer choice D makes the most logical sense in terms of the question context"
    ],
    "url": "https://www.examtopics.com/discussions/comptia/view/72981-exam-sk0-005-topic-1-question-32-discussion/"
  },
  {
    "exam_code": "sk0-005",
    "topic": 1,
    "question_num": 33,
    "question": "A server is performing slowly, and users are reporting issues connecting to the application on that server. Upon investigation, the server administrator notices several unauthorized services running on that server that are successfully communicating to an external site. Which of the following are MOST likely causing the issue? (Choose two.)",
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "Adware is installed on the users' devices",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "The firewall rule for the server is misconfigured",
        "is_correct": true
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "The server is infected with a virus",
        "is_correct": true
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "Intrusion detection is enabled on the network",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "E",
        "text": "Unnecessary services are disabled on the server",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "F",
        "text": "SELinux is enabled on the server",
        "is_correct": false
      }
    ],
    "suggested_answers": [
      "B",
      "C"
    ],
    "answers": [
      "To me, this seems like a clear case of C&C (command and control) someone hacked into the server, and it is now calling home. The hacker may have changed firewall rules to allow the server to call home, or they have have been unsecure to begin with. The problem is not the users computers having adware, the server is what is running slow, causing a denial of availability for the users. Server has malware. and server is calling home, a clear B & C for me. Have gotten Pentest+ Cysa+ Security+ Network+, most of the answers in these questions (by the community) are pretty bad. study this stuff for yourself, don't depend on the answers in the discussions. Just my opinion.",
      "IRL applications, I'd say more on B and C. Can't say adware for sure. Malware yes. but I would point more toward spyware if anything",
      "B&C are correct. \"TheITStudent\" explanation is right on.",
      "\"A server is performing slowly, ... several unauthorized services running on that server that are successfully communicating to an external site.\" End user issues are a by product of the server issues.\nI agree with TheITStudent's explanation with C&C",
      "Another Keyword CompTIA trick.. MOST like cause of the issue:\n\nAdware is a type of malware, so if you choosing a virus as the possible cause you might as well choose adware as another possible cause, before misconfigured firewall.",
      "why would firewall rules change? how is E the problem? harding is done before productions, so you have to re-harden your services... what if you shutdown the service to that application?\nWhy not check first to make sure workstations aren't infected with malware/adaware that could infect the server or spread through the network or to other users??..... Why is the server responding slowly and has external unknow communication..? Maybe workstations communicating with external port/application??\n\nI like provided answers.",
      "I would say B and C\nAdware on the users' machine is a large assumption. If everyone is affected it's unlikely that everyone has adware, and more likely that the server has a virus slowing it down.\nThe server most likely got the virus from open ports i.e. misconfigured firewall.",
      "C and E are correct"
    ],
    "url": "https://www.examtopics.com/discussions/comptia/view/73214-exam-sk0-005-topic-1-question-33-discussion/"
  },
  {
    "exam_code": "sk0-005",
    "topic": 1,
    "question_num": 34,
    "question": "A server technician is configuring the IP address on a newly installed server. The documented configuration specifies using an IP address of 10.20.10.15 and a default gateway of 10.20.10.254. Which of the following subnet masks would be appropriate for this setup?",
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "255.255.255.0",
        "is_correct": true
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "255.255.255.128",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "255.255.255.240",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "255.255.255.254",
        "is_correct": false
      }
    ],
    "suggested_answers": [
      "A"
    ],
    "answers": [
      "255.255.255.0",
      "10.20.10.15 and 10.20.10.254 should be in the same subnet\n255.255.255.0 must be the subnet mask",
      "255.255.255.0",
      "Answer is A. If you do not know this, please take Net+ next.",
      "255.255.255.0",
      "255.255.255.0 or 10.20.10.0/24",
      "I'd go with A.",
      "specifies using an IP address of 10.20.10.15 and a default gateway of 10.20.10.254, would mean you would need 256 addresses, you would lose 2 addresses, giving you 254. So you would us CIDR /24 putting you at submask 255.255.255.0",
      "A is the correct answer",
      "For both devices to be in the same subnet then it needs to include the entire range",
      "WTF? Do you really think that is right answer? I tried to use your answer and guess what?\nNumber of Usable Hosts: 0 - ROTFL\n\nIPv4 Subnet Calculator\nResult\n\nIP Address: 10.20.10.15\nNetwork Address: 10.20.10.14\nUsable Host IP Range: NA\nBroadcast Address: 10.20.10.15\nTotal Number of Hosts: 2\nNumber of Usable Hosts: 0\nSubnet Mask: 255.255.255.254",
      "you really think that is right answer? I tried to use your answer and guess what?\nNumber of Usable Hosts: 0 - ROTFL\n\nIPv4 Subnet Calculator\nResult\n\nIP Address: 10.20.10.15\nNetwork Address: 10.20.10.14\nUsable Host IP Range: NA\nBroadcast Address: 10.20.10.15\nTotal Number of Hosts: 2\nNumber of Usable Hosts: 0\nSubnet Mask: 255.255.255.254"
    ],
    "url": "https://www.examtopics.com/discussions/comptia/view/70454-exam-sk0-005-topic-1-question-34-discussion/"
