  {
    "exam_code": "sk0-005",
    "topic": 1,
    "question_num": 36,
    "question": "A large number of connections to port 80 is discovered while reviewing the log files on a server. The server is not functioning as a web server. Which of the following represent the BEST immediate actions to prevent unauthorized server access? (Choose two.)",
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "Audit all group privileges and permissions",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "Run a checksum tool against all the files on the server",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "Stop all unneeded services and block the ports on the firewall",
        "is_correct": true
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "Initialize a port scan on the server to identify open ports",
        "is_correct": true
      },
      {
        "letter": "E",
        "text": "Enable port forwarding on port 80",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "F",
        "text": "Install a NIDS on the server to prevent network intrusions",
        "is_correct": false
      }
    ],
    "suggested_answers": [
      "C",
      "D"
    ],
    "answers": [
      "Auditing isnt preventative. It is Detective. CD are the correct answers.",
      "You want to identify all open ports and disable the unneeded ones. Only way to do that is with a port scan. Running an audit is not an immediate preventative measure",
      "Disable Unused Services/Close Unneeded Ports\nAny services that are not required on the server should be disabled. Only those required for the server to perform its role in the network should be left on. The easiest way to do this is to install a host firewall on the system and adopt a “disable by default” policy with respect to services by closing the port used for the service. Then manually enable any you need.",
      "Okay, I think C and D. Here's why. For answer C, you can block port 80 at the firewall. Done! Now for answer D, running port scan on server will identify other unauthorized open ports. The answer can't be F because, it states NIDS, and the question is only focus on a single server. Which means if anything, you would install a HIDS to detect intrusions. Boom!",
      "Lets talk about why the server is not functioning as a web server and what is it functioning as? File server? Directory Service? Doesn't state... Why is port 80 open if this server is not functioning as a web server? Maybe insider threat? rough employee? someone with elevated or admin rights messing with the server? horrible question and a trick.",
      "Agree with others I'd go with C and D. A is definitely a possibility and CompTIA are the masters of word trickery.",
      "One of the tricks used on a compromised system is to utilize port 80 for communication. Regardless of what it's typically used for (http), any service can run on any port you configure it to run on. Threat actors will use it because it's a port that is commonly used for web traffic and, as such, will likely not be blocked by the firewall.\n\nNow, on most computers, ports below 1024 are privileged ports that require an super user account for those services to bind to said ports. However, if the server is compromised, it's likely that the threat actors already have super user access to that system.",
      "You would need to do A, to ensure nothing has been changed, but it won't prevent access and that is the question.",
      "C and maybe F are the immediate actions you would take. A is not an immediate action",
      "And D would take too long"
    ],
    "url": "https://www.examtopics.com/discussions/comptia/view/73216-exam-sk0-005-topic-1-question-36-discussion/"
  },
  {
    "exam_code": "sk0-005",
    "topic": 1,
    "question_num": 37,
    "question": "A company is running an application on a file server. A security scan reports the application has a known vulnerability. Which of the following would be the company's BEST course of action?",
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "Upgrade the application package",
        "is_correct": true
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "Tighten the rules on the firewall",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "Install antivirus software",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "Patch the server OS",
        "is_correct": false
      }
    ],
    "suggested_answers": [
      "A"
    ],
    "answers": [
      "A is the obvious answer",
      "first thing you would do.",
      "god help me with my choice /:",
      "I dont like word UPGRADE in ansver A, what it means, does update even fix problem or make it even worse?\nIf they used patch or at least update.\nI stick to D as \"sometimes\" server vendor found out some app veaknes-es add provide security for their OS. Microsoft for their OS also distribute patches for non MS software.",
      "The answer is D. When you patch the server, you are updating the applications.",
      "aaaaaa",
      "Why would you UPGRADE the application package? If the word was \"update\" then the answer is A. But the only answer that has update is D....",
      "Install Latest Patches\nDon't forget about the applications that may be running on the server. Applications can also be attacked by hackers. That's why software vendors are also periodically issuing security updates. As security issues are reported, they respond by fixing the software. For Windows applications, these updates can accompany the operating system updates if you choose to enable them.\n\nOther applications may be more of a challenge, but it's hard to find vendors today that don't either automatically send and install the updates or, at the very least, notify you that one is available.",
      "How is the answer A., but the first thing you said was install the latest patches.",
      "Why would you replace the door if its your doorknob that is broken? replace the doorknob...",
      "I meant have to replace my city, if my door knob is broken it's the BEST cause of action..@CompTIA"
    ],
    "url": "https://www.examtopics.com/discussions/comptia/view/73217-exam-sk0-005-topic-1-question-37-discussion/"
  },
  {
    "exam_code": "sk0-005",
    "topic": 1,
    "question_num": 38,
    "question": "A technician runs top on a dual-core server and notes the following conditions: top `\"- 14:32:27, 364 days, 14 users load average 60.5 12.4 13.6\nWhich of the following actions should the administrator take?",
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "Schedule a mandatory reboot of the server",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "Wait for the load average to come back down on its own",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "Identify the runaway process or processes",
        "is_correct": true
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "Request that users log off the server",
        "is_correct": false
      }
    ],
    "suggested_answers": [
      "C"
    ],
    "answers": [
      "the answer is C for sure",
      "no cccccccccccccccc correct",
      "aaaaaaaaaaaaaaa",
      "Well, you don't want to reboot unless it's absolutely necessary. So no. And why would I boot users who have nothing to do with the problem? Find the problem process, and maybe the associated user, and address it from there.",
      "Load Average in Linux is a metric that is used by Linux users to keep track of system resources. It also helps you monitor how the system resources are engaged.\nTo understand the Load Average in Linux, we need to know what do we define as load. In a Linux system, the load is a measure of CPU utilization at any given moment.\nIt refers to the number of processes which are either currently being executed by the CPU or are waiting for execution.\nAn idle system has a load of 0. With each process that is being executed or is on the waitlist, the load increases by 1.\n\nOccasionally a process will stop responding to the system and run wild. These processes ignore their scheduling priority and insist on taking up 100% of the CPU. Because other processes can only get limited access to the CPU, the machine begins to run very slowly.\n\nhttps://www.digitalocean.com/community/tutorials/load-average-in-linux\nhttps://docs.cs.byu.edu/doku.php?id=runaway-processes",
      "(cont.)\nHow can I identify a runaway process on my computer?\nThe 'w' command at the terminal will print out a list of current users of a machine, and it will tell you the machine's “load average.” The load average of a machine is related to how much input/output the machine has to do. A load average of 1 is a machine under full load. Anything over 1 is extremely high and means that the machine is getting behind on its processing. If your machine has a load average near or over 1, and you are not running anything really resource intensive on the machine, then you probably have a runaway process sapping your machine's processing power.\n\nhttps://www.digitalocean.com/community/tutorials/load-average-in-linux\nhttps://docs.cs.byu.edu/doku.php?id=runaway-processes",
      "Users logging off does not seem to be the answer here. I would identify the processes and possibly reboot the server depending on what I find going wrong with the TOP command."
    ],
    "url": "https://www.examtopics.com/discussions/comptia/view/82161-exam-sk0-005-topic-1-question-38-discussion/"
  },
  {
    "exam_code": "sk0-005",
    "topic": 1,
    "question_num": 39,
    "question": "A technician needs to set up a server backup method for some systems. The company's management team wants to have quick restores but minimize the amount of backup media required. Which of the following are the BEST backup methods to use to support the management's priorities? (Choose two.)",
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "Differential",
        "is_correct": true
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "Synthetic full",
        "is_correct": true
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "Archive",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "Full",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "E",
        "text": "Incremental",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "F",
        "text": "Open file",
        "is_correct": false
      }
    ],
    "suggested_answers": [
      "A",
      "B"
    ],
    "answers": [
      "A. Differential\n\nBacks up all changes since the last full backup.\nRestore requires last full backup + latest differential.\n✅ Faster restore than incremental and uses less media than multiple full backups.\n\n\n\nB. Synthetic full\n\nCreates a full backup by combining previous full and incremental backups without re-reading all data from the source.\n✅ Saves media and speeds up restore because it behaves like a full backup.",
      "Synthetic Full\nA synthetic backup is a full backup performed by synthesizing (combining) the data from the previous full backup (either a regular full backup for the first backup, or the previous synthetic full backup) and the periodic incremental backups. The incremental backups are the only files that need to be transferred during replication, greatly reducing the bandwidth needed for off-site replication.",
      "Chatgpt",
      "The answers are AB check out this link https://aws.amazon.com/compare/the-difference-between-incremental-differential-and-other-backups/#:~:text=A%20differential%20backup%20strategy%20only,changes%20since%20the%20last%20backup.",
      "An incremental backup is a backup of changes since a specified marker (usually a\nfull backup) and resets the archive bit. Backups are quicker than differentials, but\nthe restoral process is slower (must use each backup job after the full).\n\nThis means we want differential and synthetic full",
      "Incremental: This method backs up only the changes since the last backup, which minimizes the amount of backup media required and can be performed quickly.\nSynthetic full: This method creates a full backup by combining the last full backup with the changes since the last backup, which minimizes the amount of backup media required and provides a quick restore.",
      "I am going with B&E",
      "Differential: Takes less space than full backups, Faster restoration than incremental backups\nSynthetic full\nFull: Quick restore time, take up large amounts of unnecessary storage space\nIncremental: Time-consuming restoration since data must be pieced together from multiple backups\n\nhttps://www.unitrends.com/blog/types-of-backup-full-incremental-differential\nhttps://gomindsight.com/insights/blog/four-data-backup-methods-it-resiliency/",
      "Key word 'but minimize the amount of backup media required\"\nSo It Incremental and synthetic",
      "I'd go with provided answers.\n\nFull Slow backup, fast restore\n\nFull+Incremental Fast backup, slow restore (compared to\nFull+Differential)\n\nFull+Differential Slow backup, fast restore (compared to\nFull+Incremental)\n\nReference: The Official CompTIA Server+ Study Guide (Exam SK0-005)",
      "B is quickest and E would take the least amount of time",
      "E - Incremental would increase the amount of media and take longer time to restore. Faster backup time.\nA - Differential would decrease the amount of media and take shorter time to restore. Longer backup time.\nB - Synthetic Full - fast restore\nSynthetic Full/Differential meets managements requirements.",
      "Differential (and Incremental) would actually *increase* the amount of tapes needed in the event of a restore, because you'd have to restore the most recent Full backup as well as the most recent Differential (or in the case of Incrementals, all incrementals since the most recent Full)"
    ],
    "url": "https://www.examtopics.com/discussions/comptia/view/73219-exam-sk0-005-topic-1-question-39-discussion/"
  },
  {
    "exam_code": "sk0-005",
    "topic": 1,
    "question_num": 40,
    "question": "Ann, an administrator, is configuring a two-node cluster that will be deployed. To check the cluster's functionality, she shuts down the active node. Cluster behavior is as expected, and the passive node is now active. Ann powers on the server again and wants to return to the original configuration. Which of the following cluster features will allow Ann to complete this task?",
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "Heartbeat",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "Failback",
        "is_correct": true
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "Redundancy",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "Load balancing",
        "is_correct": false
      }
    ],
    "suggested_answers": [
      "B"
    ],
    "answers": [
      "When you research server clustering, you frequently hear the terms failover and failback. Failover refers to the failure of the active service provider node and passive node's taking over of its responsibilities. At that point, the server that was the passive node becomes the active node. It services clients and handles the cluster’s duties. Once the original active node returns to service, you may set it to act as the new passive node, or you may shift the services back to it, causing it to take back its active node role and relegating the formerly passive server to its original role. The term failback refers to the service returning to the original active node.",
      "Failback"
    ],
    "url": "https://www.examtopics.com/discussions/comptia/view/71867-exam-sk0-005-topic-1-question-40-discussion/"
  },
  {
    "exam_code": "sk0-005",
    "topic": 1,
    "question_num": 41,
    "question": "Which of the following policies would be BEST to deter a brute-force login attack?",
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "Password complexity",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "Password reuse",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "Account age threshold",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "Account lockout threshold",
        "is_correct": true
      }
    ],
    "suggested_answers": [
      "D"
    ],
    "answers": [
      "The BEST policy to deter a brute-force login attack is:\nD. Account lockout threshold\nWhy?\n\nA brute-force attack relies on repeatedly guessing passwords.\nAccount lockout threshold sets a limit on the number of failed login attempts before the account is locked, making brute-force attacks impractical.",
      "I wonder who is choosing the wrong answers in this website for this course?",
      "The best policy to deter a brute-force login attack is D. Account lockout threshold. This policy directly limits the number of login attempts, thereby preventing an attacker from continuously attempting to guess the correct password. Implementing an account lockout threshold makes brute-force attacks impractical by significantly increasing the time required to successfully guess a password.",
      "Because a policy of locking out accounts after a certain number of failed login attempts can effectively deter brute-force attacks. This is because the attacker will be unable to continue trying different passwords once the account has been locked out, making it more difficult to gain unauthorized access.",
      "Attacker cannot continue to try different passwords if the account gets locked out. That's actually from A+.",
      "Dis the correct answer, locking out the user or attacker after few tries will prevent that",
      "D Lockout policy is the BEST option\nThe other options create a strong password policy"
    ],
    "url": "https://www.examtopics.com/discussions/comptia/view/82468-exam-sk0-005-topic-1-question-41-discussion/"
  },
  {
    "exam_code": "sk0-005",
    "topic": 1,
    "question_num": 42,
    "question": "A technician needs to install a Type 1 hypervisor on a server. The server has SD card slots, a SAS controller, and a SATA controller, and it is attached to a NAS.\nOn which of the following drive types should the technician install the hypervisor?",
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "SD card",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "NAS drive",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "SATA drive",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "SAS drive",
        "is_correct": true
      }
    ],
    "suggested_answers": [
      "D"
    ],
    "answers": [
      "D is the correct answer. SATA would not be used on a server because its not server grade storage",
      "I agree with Dion79... going with SD card. Specifically because the questions states that the server has sd card slot available, in that case, these are there FOR THAT PARTICULAR PURPOSE... see: https://serverfault.com/questions/823791/what-is-the-use-case-of-a-sd-slot-on-motherboard",
      "A. SD card\nSometimes used for hypervisor installation in embedded systems.\nPros: easy, inexpensive\nCons: slow, limited write endurance, not ideal for production servers.\n❌ Not best for a production server hypervisor.\n\nB. NAS drive\nNetwork storage (NFS, iSCSI) is not local.\nHypervisor OS should not rely on network storage to boot. ❌\n\nC. SATA drive\nAcceptable for OS, but slower than SAS, especially under heavy load.\nCan work, but not ideal for enterprise environments.\n\nD. SAS drive ✅\nHigh-speed, enterprise-grade storage, designed for server workloads.\nFast, reliable, and supports high IOPS — perfect for a Type 1 hypervisor.",
      "✅ A. SD card\nExplanation:\nA Type 1 hypervisor (bare-metal hypervisor) like VMware ESXi or Microsoft Hyper-V is installed directly on the hardware. It typically requires very little disk space and minimal IOPS since the actual virtual machines and their workloads are run from separate storage.\n\nSD cards are commonly used by OEMs (like Dell, HPE, etc.) to host hypervisors because:\n\nThe hypervisor has low write requirements.\n\nIt leaves SATA and SAS drives free for storing virtual machines.\n\nThey are cost-effective for this use.\n\nNote: If persistent logging or frequent writes are required, some admins configure logs to go to other storage (e.g., a data store on SAS or SATA) to avoid wearing out the SD card.",
      "Going with SAS over SATA here, I mean you have it so why not?",
      "SD cards typically do not support RAID configurations. A tenant of server management is redundancy.",
      "I agree with Dion79... going with SD card. An SD card is an ideal choice for installing a Type 1 hypervisor because. It provides a dedicated, local boot device for the hypervisor.\nIt separates the hypervisor from the main storage, allowing for easier management and upgrades.\nMany enterprise-grade servers come with internal SD card slots specifically designed for hypervisor installation.\nWhile SATA or SAS drives could technically be used, they are better suited for VM storage and data. The NAS drive is not a suitable option for a Type 1 hypervisor installation, as it's not directly attached to the server hardware and could introduce performance and reliability issues",
      "Option D SAS DRIVE is the correct answer. The question clearly says that \"On which of the following drive types should the technician install the hypervisor?\". SD card is not a drive type and it is not normally used as source to run an OS. Running a Type 1 hypervisor on an SD card in a production environment is not ideal. They can be used to host a lightweight embedded system.",
      "C is the correct answer.\nIn many common environments, a hypervisor can be run on SATA without any problems.\nHypervisors themselves usually do not require large storage capacities or extreme speeds, so SATA drives are often sufficient.",
      "It is D, if they said it had SD card slots with SD cards installed it would be A but it has a SAS controller attached to a NAS and empty SD cards so you use SAS.",
      "For installing a Type 1 hypervisor on a server, the technician should install it on:\n\nA. SD card\n\nExplanation:\n\nInstalling the hypervisor on an SD card is a common practice for Type 1 hypervisors. SD cards offer a convenient and cost-effective solution for booting the hypervisor directly from the server hardware. This approach allows for efficient utilization of internal storage resources while keeping the hypervisor separate from the storage used for virtual machines.",
      "aaaaaaaa",
      "D (as others mentioned) is correct",
      "Agree with Dion79 Correct Answer is D",
      "Assuming SD Slots are internal. If so, then Internal SD Slots are recommended.",
      "D is the answer",
      "A & D are both possible answers. All components described in this example are internal. Assuming SD Slots are internal. If so, then Internal SD Slots are recommended. \"The Internal SanDisk (SD) slots remain enabled because the ESXI hypervisor runs on dual SD cards inside the server chassis\".\n\nReference: Lesson 9 under Configure Server Hardening. 31 of 93 CompTIA Server+ Final Assessment."
    ],
    "url": "https://www.examtopics.com/discussions/comptia/view/73220-exam-sk0-005-topic-1-question-42-discussion/"
  },
