        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "RTO",
        "is_correct": true
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "MTTR",
        "is_correct": false
      }
    ],
    "suggested_answers": [
      "C"
    ],
    "answers": [
      "Recovery Time Objective (RTO)\nThis is the shortest time period after a disaster or disruptive event within which a resource or function must be restored in order to avoid unacceptable consequences. RTO assumes that an acceptable period of downtime exists."
    ],
    "url": "https://www.examtopics.com/discussions/comptia/view/87190-exam-sk0-005-topic-1-question-55-discussion/"
  },
  {
    "exam_code": "sk0-005",
    "topic": 1,
    "question_num": 56,
    "question": "Which of the following actions should a server administrator take once a new backup scheme has been configured?",
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "Overwrite the backups",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "Clone the configuration",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "Run a restore test",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "Check the media integrity",
        "is_correct": true
      }
    ],
    "suggested_answers": [
      "D"
    ],
    "answers": [
      "I think D is correct. Only a scheme has been created. No backups have been taken yet so you cannot so a test restore.",
      "This should be C",
      "I'd go with C as well. Running a restore will validate integrity and if backup was successful.",
      "The correct answer is: C. Run a restore test\n\n🔍 Explanation:\nOnce a new backup scheme has been configured, the most critical step is to verify that you can successfully restore data from the backup. Backups are only useful if they can be restored reliably when needed.\n\n✅ Why C. Run a restore test is correct:\nIt validates the backup process.\n\nEnsures that data can be recovered in case of an actual failure or disaster.\n\nHelps identify misconfigurations in the backup process early.",
      "By doing C you are testing D and more.",
      ". If a new backup scheme has been configured but no actual backups have been taken yet, then checking the media integrity (option D) would indeed be a suitable action to take. This ensures that the backup media (such as tapes, disks, etc.) is reliable and can be trusted for future backups. Running a restore test (option C) would typically be done after actual backups have been taken to ensure that the restoration process works correctly.",
      "Once a new backup scheme has been configured, a server administrator should run a restore test. A restore test involves restoring data from the backup to ensure that the backup is working correctly and that the data can be successfully restored in the event of a disaster. Running a restore test is an important step in verifying that the backup scheme is properly configured and that the backups are reliable.",
      "cccccccccccccc",
      "I am going with Answer D. Sure, a test (C) is also needed, and may very well work, but you need to verify media integrity and confirm it is not in need of replacement. The very first thing in the All-In-One Server+ book \"after\" mentioning picking out a scheme (backup solution), is to verify media integrity.",
      "D seems correct."
    ],
    "url": "https://www.examtopics.com/discussions/comptia/view/73460-exam-sk0-005-topic-1-question-56-discussion/"
  },
  {
    "exam_code": "sk0-005",
    "topic": 1,
    "question_num": 57,
    "question": "A systems administrator is performing maintenance on 12 Windows servers that are in different racks at a large datacenter. Which of the following would allow the administrator to perform maintenance on all 12 servers without having to physically be at each server? (Choose two.)",
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "Remote desktop",
        "is_correct": true
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "IP KVM",
        "is_correct": true
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "A console connection",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "A virtual administration console",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "E",
        "text": "Remote drive access",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "F",
        "text": "A crash cart",
        "is_correct": false
      }
    ],
    "suggested_answers": [
      "A",
      "B"
    ],
    "answers": [
      "Definitely B and D… Remote Desktop offers full control over the target computer, whereas admin consoles allow administrators to manage specific settings and components of various systems. Virtual Administration Consoles are designed for managing larger IT environments and can be more scalable than Remote Desktop for large-scale deployments.",
      "I would go B & D here. D, because I use this everyday at work managing 20 servers with vSphere. B, because we use this as well on some of our older equipment. I also use A as well but less often.",
      "D: is eliminated, because it only applies to virtual servers. However, there is no indication of this in the question, which explicitly refers to servers that could be physical, i.e., not VMs.\n\nE: Remote drive access\nLets you mount or share drives remotely, but not administer the system fully.\n\nF: A crash cart\nPhysical rolling station with monitor/keyboard used onsite — requires being at each rack.\n\nThats leaves A & B as the correct answers.",
      "A,B is correct answers."
    ],
    "url": "https://www.examtopics.com/discussions/comptia/view/108183-exam-sk0-005-topic-1-question-57-discussion/"
  },
  {
    "exam_code": "sk0-005",
    "topic": 1,
    "question_num": 58,
    "question": "A server administrator is experiencing difficulty configuring MySQL on a Linux server. The administrator issues the getenforce command and receives the following output:\n># Enforcing\nWhich of the following commands should the administrator issue to configure MySQL successfully?",
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "setenforce 0",
        "is_correct": true
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "setenforce permissive",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "setenforce 1",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "setenforce disabled",
        "is_correct": false
      }
    ],
    "suggested_answers": [
      "A"
    ],
    "answers": [
      "The getenforce command is used to check the current status of SELinux (Security-Enhanced Linux) on a system. The output \"Enforcing\" indicates that SELinux is currently in enforcing mode, which may restrict certain operations.\n\nTo configure MySQL successfully, the administrator can temporarily set SELinux to permissive mode, which allows policy violations to be logged but not enforced. The correct command for this is:\n\nB. setenforce permissive\n\nThis command will set SELinux to permissive mode, allowing the administrator to identify and address any SELinux policy violations related to MySQL without enforcing strict policies. After configuring MySQL, it's generally a good practice to set SELinux back to enforcing mode for enhanced security, using the command:\n\nC. setenforce 1\n\nThis will set SELinux back to enforcing mode",
      "A. setenforce 0. This command sets SELinux to \"Permissive\" mode. In Permissive mode, SELinux does not deny access but logs AVC (Access Vector Cache) messages. It's useful for troubleshooting and understanding how SELinux would behave without actually enforcing any denials.",
      "The answer is A\nSetenforce 0: to change to permissive mode\nSetenforce 1: to change to enforcing mode",
      "\"setenforce 0\" sets SELinux to \"disabled\" mode, which completely turns off SELinux enforcement.\n\n\"setenforce permissive\" sets SELinux to \"permissive\" mode, which allows SELinux to log policy violations but does not block any actions.\n\n\"setenforce 1\" sets the SELinux enforcement to \"enforcing\" mode, which means SELinux policy rules are actively enforced.\n\n\"setenforce disabled\" sets the SELinux enforcement to \"disabled\" mode, which means SELinux policy rules are not enforced at all, and the system falls back to standard Unix permissions."
    ],
    "url": "https://www.examtopics.com/discussions/comptia/view/74633-exam-sk0-005-topic-1-question-58-discussion/"
  },
  {
    "exam_code": "sk0-005",
    "topic": 1,
    "question_num": 59,
    "question": "Which of the following backup types only records changes to the data blocks on a virtual machine?",
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "Differential",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "Snapshot",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "Incremental",
        "is_correct": true
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "Synthetic full",
        "is_correct": false
      }
    ],
    "suggested_answers": [
      "C"
    ],
    "answers": [
      "Chatgpt and other websites are choosing C: Snapshots",
      "My bad guys, its Incremental",
      "I don't like how this is written, because it could be incremental or differential, in my thinking. Yes, incremental will only copy the changed data blocks, but then it ALSO resets the archive bit.... Where differential will only backup what has changed since the last time the bit was reset.",
      "I think a keyword is \"only records changes to the data blocks\". The provided answer could be correct.",
      "https://helpcenter.veeam.com/docs/backup/hyperv/changed_block_tracking.html?ver=110",
      "https://www.ibm.com/docs/en/spfve/8.1.2?topic=machines-vm-backups-resilient-change-tracking-rct\n\nTricky question.. Provided answer maybe correct I was thinking snapshot but question seem smore geeared to incremential."
    ],
    "url": "https://www.examtopics.com/discussions/comptia/view/74635-exam-sk0-005-topic-1-question-59-discussion/"
  },
  {
    "exam_code": "sk0-005",
    "topic": 1,
    "question_num": 60,
    "question": "Which of the following server types would benefit MOST from the use of a load balancer?",
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
