  {
    "exam_code": "sk0-005",
    "topic": 1,
    "question_num": 61,
    "question": "A company uses a hot-site, disaster-recovery model. Which of the following types of data replication is required?",
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "Asynchronous",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "Incremental",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "Application consistent",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "Constant",
        "is_correct": true
      }
    ],
    "suggested_answers": [
      "D"
    ],
    "answers": [
      "There is no such thing as Constant Replication, it's proper name is Synchronous replication.\n\nSynchronous replication ensures that data is written to primary and alternate\nlocations without delay; this results in an up-to-date mirror copy of data\nbetween a primary and a hot site and is often done in the background\nautomatically. Asynchronous replication includes a slight delay before data is\nwritten to alternate sites; as a result, this is less expensive than synchronous\nsolutions, but it can cause problems with applications depending upon\ndatabase consistency. A B C are all not valid options for me so i'd go with D. Typicaly vague CompTIA question.",
      "Explanation:\nConstant Replication\nThe primary system replicates data changed data blocks continually. Constant replication is also\nreferred to as \"continuous replication.\" The replication process occurs in the background,\npermitting users to access the data without interruption.\nConstant replication is different than regular backups, where files must be closed to be\nduplicated.",
      "First i thought A should be the answer but: Asynchronous data replication is typically associated with a warm-site disaster recovery model, not a hot-site model. In a hot-site disaster recovery setup, synchronous (or, in this case; constant) replication is more commonly used, where the secondary site is fully operational and mirrors data in real-time from the primary site. so D is correct.",
      "Answer is D.",
      "A. Asynchronous.\nAsynchronous data replication is typically used in a hot-site disaster recovery model. This is because asynchronous replication does not require an immediate update to the remote site, allowing for a slight delay in replication. This delay is usually acceptable in a hot-site model, where the remote site is already configured and ready to take over in case of a disaster. Option A is the correct answer.",
      "Answer is D. Answer A technically runs the risk of data loss.",
      "Constant.\nA hot site requires the same data that's at your primary site, requiring constant replication.",
      "Constant\nWhen constant replication is in use, each time a change is made (data addition, deletion, change, etc.) at the primary site, the same change is written to the secondary site. This results in the secondary site being constantly up-to-date and is generally an expensive option.\n\nCompTIA Server+ Study Guide Exam SK0-005 Chapter 9 Disaster Recovery",
      "Does CompTia use its own exam guides to write the tests? According to their book, chapter 8 page 329, the two types of replication used for a hot site are synchronous and asynchronous. There is no mention anywhere in the text about \"constant\" replication. I hope they are not sourcing test material from outside of the book that they produced for the purpose of learning these topics.",
      "Read page 328 at the very top. It helped me",
      "Constant Replication\nThe primary system replicates data changed data blocks continually. Constant\nreplication is also referred to as \"continuous replication.\" The replication process\noccurs in the background, permitting users to access the data without interruption.\nConstant replication is different than regular backups, where files must be closed\nto be duplicated.",
      "Hot sites are actively updated asynchronously. This occurs in real time, and provides a near-mirror image of your production site on your target systems. Standby latencies for hot sites are typically only milliseconds in length, resulting in little to no downtime during failover.\nAsynchronous replication is also commonly used in virtual machines (VMs): Some hypervisors include asynchronous replication to allow entire VMs to be replicated to a remote location so the VM can fail over to that location in the event of a disaster. Another common use case for asynchronous replication is storage snapshots for continuous data protection.\n\nhttps://protostechnologies.com/blog/disaster-recovery/recovery-site-hot-warm-or-cold/\n\nhttps://blog.purestorage.com/purely-informational/synchronous-replication-vs-aynchronous-replication/#",
      "D must be the answer",
      "\"Constant\" isn't a replication method. The methods are synchronous or asynchronous.\nAsynchronous replicates the data at a scheduled time and reduces bandwith, compared to synchronous which replicates date instantly.\nSynchronous would be best if you have two live sites. Asynchronous would be used for a hot-site which would only be used for a failover.",
      "It is a replication method...., according to CompTIA. It's a trick questions and I'd probably still pick Constant.. Page 249 Topic 11c (Managing Service and Data Availability - The Office CompTIA Server+ Study Guide Exam Sk0-005)\nConstant, Periodic, Asynchronous, Synchronous, Application Consistent, File Locking, Mirroring, BiDi...",
      "Constant replication over a high-speed network link can ensure\nfast data copies and data consistency across locations.",
      "The primary system replicates data changed data blocks continually. Constant replication is also referred to as \"continuous replication.\" The replication process occurs in the background, permitting users to access the data without interruption. Constant replication is different than regular backups, where files must be closed to be duplicated.\n\nasynchronous replication, data is sent to the primary storage (a database, for example). Confirmation that the data was written is sent to the client machine. The data is then replicated to the second database server, and it responds to the first database server with a confirmation. The problem arises if the primary server fails. The secondary may not actually have the replicated data, but the client computer believes that it does. The data is then lost.\n\nReference: The Official CompTIA Server+ Study Guide"
    ],
    "url": "https://www.examtopics.com/discussions/comptia/view/73705-exam-sk0-005-topic-1-question-61-discussion/"
  },
  {
    "exam_code": "sk0-005",
    "topic": 1,
    "question_num": 62,
    "question": "A technician is unable to access a server's package repository internally or externally. Which of the following are the MOST likely reasons? (Choose two.)",
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "The server has an architecture mismatch",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "The system time is not synchronized",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "The technician does not have sufficient privileges",
        "is_correct": true
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "The external firewall is blocking access",
        "is_correct": true
      },
      {
        "letter": "E",
        "text": "The default gateway is incorrect",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "F",
        "text": "The local system log file is full",
        "is_correct": false
      }
    ],
    "suggested_answers": [
      "C",
      "D"
    ],
    "answers": [
      "C. The technician does not have sufficient privileges to access the package repository.\nD. The external firewall is blocking access to the package repository.\nTherefore, options C and D are the correct answers.\nOption A (architecture mismatch) may cause issues with software installation or updates, but it is not related to the server's package repository access.\nOption B (system time not synchronized) may cause authentication issues but is unlikely to affect package repository access.\nOption E (incorrect default gateway) may affect network connectivity, but it is not directly related to package repository access.\nOption F (local system log file full) may affect logging and monitoring but is unlikely to affect package repository access.",
      "The question said INTERNALLY and externally so D is incorrect about the external firewall being issue.",
      "Hello Dear Replicant, read the question again as it says INTERNALLY or EXTERNALLY. so it means both",
      "D. The external firewall is blocking access\nE. The default gateway is incorrect\n\nHere's why:\nThe technician is unable to access the package repository, internally or externally — meaning the issue could be related to network access or routing problems, not necessarily something like permissions or logs.",
      "Hello\nFor people choosing D & E, both answers are correct for external access issues but what about the internal access issue? out of A,B,C and F, C is the most appropriate answer for not having access INTERNALLY to a repository for a technician. In this context I think C and D are the right answers where D emphasizes more on the network side rather accessing files in the server.",
      "C. The technician does not have sufficient privileges\nIf the technician does not have sufficient privileges to access the repository, the internal repository cannot be accessed. For internal access, this is most likely due to the technician's lack of authority.\n\nD. The external firewall is blocking access\nIf access to the external repository is not possible, it is most likely that a firewall is blocking traffic.",
      "The most likely reasons a technician is unable to access a server's package repository both internally and externally are:\n\nD. The external firewall is blocking access - Firewalls can block incoming and outgoing traffic. If the firewall is configured to block access to the package repository, the technician won't be able to access it.\n\nE. The default gateway is incorrect - The default gateway is responsible for routing traffic outside the local network. If it's incorrectly configured, the server might not be able to reach external resources, including external package repositories.\n\nWhile some of the other options might cause issues with specific operations on a server, they are less likely to be the direct cause of inability to access a package repository both internally and externally.",
      "from my expirience C it is sure right from other gateway misconfiguration can happen if there is some VLAN change.",
      "ddddddddddddddddddd,eeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeee",
      "A, C should be the answer.",
      "I also think C could be a valid answer as well... if not having the correct permissions can also cause this."
    ],
    "url": "https://www.examtopics.com/discussions/comptia/view/72009-exam-sk0-005-topic-1-question-62-discussion/"
  },
  {
    "exam_code": "sk0-005",
    "topic": 1,
    "question_num": 63,
    "question": "A server administrator was asked to build a storage array with the highest possible capacity. Which of the following RAID levels should the administrator choose?",
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "RAID 0",
        "is_correct": true
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "RAID 1",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "RAID 5",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "RAID 6",
        "is_correct": false
      }
    ],
    "suggested_answers": [
      "A"
    ],
    "answers": [
      "I think that would be RAID 5. Source is here https://www.trentonsystems.com/blog/raid-levels-0-1-5-6-10-raid-types",
      "\"highest possible capacity\"\n\nRAID 0 offers highest capacity compared to the other RAIDs.",
      "Answer looks correct...\n\nhttps://www.computerweekly.com/answer/Choosing-a-RAID-level-depends-on-capacity-needs-and-data-criticality"
    ],
