  {
    "exam_code": "sk0-005",
    "topic": 1,
    "question_num": 67,
    "question": "A technician is connecting a server's secondary NIC to a separate network. The technician connects the cable to the switch but then does not see any link lights on the NIC. The technician confirms there is nothing wrong on the network or with the physical connection. Which of the following should the technician perform\nNEXT?",
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "Restart the server",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "Configure the network on the server",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "Enable the port on the server",
        "is_correct": true
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "Check the DHCP configuration",
        "is_correct": false
      }
    ],
    "suggested_answers": [
      "C"
    ],
    "answers": [
      "C. Enable the port on the server\nIf there’s no link light and hardware/cabling are fine, the NIC itself may be disabled in:\nThe server’s BIOS/UEFI, or\nThe operating system’s network settings (e.g., “interface down” in Linux, or disabled adapter in Windows).\nEnabling it will bring up the interface and should show link lights once active.\nThis is the correct next step.\n\nB. Configure the network on the server\nNetwork configuration (IP, gateway, etc.) happens after the NIC is active.\nIf there are no link lights, the interface likely isn’t even enabled at the OS or BIOS level yet.\nConfiguration won’t help if the NIC is currently down.\n\nA & D are wrong.\nSo the correct answer ist C.",
      "No link lights and the network configuration has been verified would suggest something is disable with the port. If it was only a configuration issue you should still see some link light activity.",
      "B, if your going through the trouble of of connecting the second NIC you should probably configure it first before connecting it to the network.",
      "C looks correct. If you disable your NIC or uninstall it (or if it is corrupted), I believe the light will go out (if you had it on prior). Restarting (A) is part of troubleshooting, B&C would not cause the light to not come on.",
      "Nothing in the question states that the technician has configured the NIC for the \"new\" network it is getting setup for.",
      "Vague question... as always! My first thoughts are that this would need to be configured... but since \"everything on the network is fine\"... whatever that means... I would guess that maybe there is port security and to turn that on... also an inference... this question stinks."
    ],
    "url": "https://www.examtopics.com/discussions/comptia/view/78030-exam-sk0-005-topic-1-question-67-discussion/"
  },
