  {
    "exam_code": "sk0-005",
    "topic": 1,
    "question_num": 74,
    "question": "A datacenter technician is attempting to troubleshoot a server that keeps crashing. The server runs normally for approximately five minutes, but then it crashes.\nAfter restoring the server to operation, the same cycle repeats. The technician confirms none of the configurations have changed, and the load on the server is steady from power-on until the crash. Which of the following will MOST likely resolve the issue?",
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "Reseating any expansion cards in the server",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "Replacing the failing hard drive",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "Reinstalling the heat sink with new thermal paste",
        "is_correct": true
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "Restoring the server from the latest full backup",
        "is_correct": false
      }
    ],
    "suggested_answers": [
      "C"
    ],
    "answers": [
      "C would be the only answer to explain why there is an issue after about 5 minutes. Just long enough for the CPU to overheat. There are over reasons (ie rouge processes) why an issue could happen over a period of time but C is only one out of this group of answers. A. doesn’t make sense how would expansion card become loose after running the server for 5 minutes? Reseating the expansion card would make more sense if the server couldn’t see the card at all.",
      "C is the correct answer.\nProblems that occur over time often indicate thermal problems.",
      "Wouldn't a server upon reaching Tj (maximum temperature) just simply circuit itself to protect it - that means the power CUTS OFF.\n\nA system crash is not equal to a sudden shutdown. I would go for the reseat, A..",
      "Wouldn't a server upon reaching Tj (maximum temperature) just simply circuit itself to protect it - that means the power CUTS OFF.\n\nA system crash is not equal to a sudden shutdown. I would go for the reseat, A..",
      "A server suddenly turning off without any power issues typically indicates overheating.\nCPUs overheating can very often cause servers to just die after being on for 2-10 minutes."
    ],
    "url": "https://www.examtopics.com/discussions/comptia/view/106541-exam-sk0-005-topic-1-question-74-discussion/"
  },
