  {
    "exam_code": "sk0-005",
    "topic": 1,
    "question_num": 81,
    "question": "Which of the following steps in the troubleshooting theory should be performed after a solution has been implemented? (Choose two.)",
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "Perform a root cause analysis",
        "is_correct": true
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "Develop a plan of action",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "Document the findings",
        "is_correct": true
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "Escalate the issue",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "E",
        "text": "Scope the issue",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "F",
        "text": "Notify the users",
        "is_correct": false
      }
    ],
    "suggested_answers": [
      "A",
      "C"
    ],
    "answers": [
      "The following list represents the basic steps in a troubleshooting methodology: \n\nIdentify the problem\nDetermine the scope of the problem\nEstablish a theory of probable cause/question the obvious\nTest the theory to determine the cause\nEstablish a plan of action\nImplement the solution or escalate the issue\nVerify full system functionality\nImplement preventive measures\nPerform a root cause analysis\nDocument findings, actions, and outcomes throughout the process.",
      "Root cause analysis is part of \"implementing preventive measure\" cause it helps you in:\nKnowing WHAT happened\nWHY it happened\nand HOW to prevent it from happening again.",
      "A&C as its obviously state in Dions list.",
      "Troubleshooting methodology order. according to CompTIA Server + Study Guide SK0-005",
      "I want to do RCA during the implementation of solutions not afterward otherwise how do you know you implemented the right fix if you weren't certain of the cause. Due to this C and F seem right to me, document and then tell people they can use it again.",
      "I also thought the answers were A and C, but from CompTIA:\n\nhttps://www.comptia.org/blog/troubleshooting-methodology\n\n1. Identify the problem\n2. Establish a theory of probable cause\n3. Test the theory to determine the cause\n4. Establish a plan of action to resolve the problem and implement the solution\n5. Verify full system functionality, and, if applicable, implement preventive measures\n6. Document findings, actions and outcomes\n\nRoot cause should be identified in step #3, and you can't do step #5 where you implement preventive measures without knowing root cause.\n\nHowever, in the real world, cause isn't necessarily root cause. Cause, in most cases, is be a byproduct of root cause. What you implement to fix the problem isn't always the same root issue that caused the problem. I've been in IT for over 30 years and I've seen many-a-cases where root cause is different than the byproduct issue that the user was dealing with. But we have to think like CompTIA and stick to their theory to the T.",
      "1 Identify the problem and determine the scope\n2 Establish a theory of probable cause (question the obvious)\n3 Test the theory to determine the cause\n4 Establish a plan of action to resolve the problem (Notify impacted users is here)\n5 Implement the solution or escalate\n6 Verify full system functionality and, if applicable, implement preventive measures\n7 Perform a root cause analysis\n8 Document findings, actions, and outcomes throughout the process",
      "Document is not a last step. You document throughout.",
      "You are correct, but final step according to CompTIA is outcome, finds, and conclusion. You need to know your final answer through all the troubleshooting and documentation that you do.",
      "Answer looks suspect. If you follow the troubleshooting methodology A&C are after Implementing the solution no escalation was needed since the technician found the solution then A - perform a root cause analysis with C documenting findings."
    ],
