  {
    "exam_code": "sk0-005",
    "topic": 1,
    "question_num": 89,
    "question": "A server administrator needs to create a new folder on a file server that only specific users can access. Which of the following BEST describes how the server administrator can accomplish this task?",
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "Create a group that includes all users and assign it to an ACL.",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "Assign individual permissions on the folder to each user.",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "Create a group that includes all users and assign the proper permissions.",
        "is_correct": true
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "Assign ownership on the folder for each user.",
        "is_correct": false
      }
    ],
    "suggested_answers": [
      "C"
    ],
    "answers": [
      "The question is confusing, only sensible answer is C assuming \"all users\" are \"specific users\"",
      "C is the correct and most efficient method.\n\nCreate a security group (e.g., “Finance_Share_Access”), add the specific authorized users, and assign folder permissions to that group.\nEasy to manage — just add/remove users from the group as needed.",
      "A Group \"Share\" is created for members to access then NTFS Group permission's are created for granular access to the folders inside the share.",
      "Always modify by group, not by user - whenever possible.",
      "Assuming the company has worldwide users ( more than 2k) and some specific users whether local or abroad, needs to access the file for their project. It is assumed that file is related to one project which should be accessed by the users who are working on the same project but may be belonged to different departments as well as cities or may be countries they living in.\nI think C is the right answer where you need to create a group named \" The Project\" so admin can easily add those users to the group to access the file",
      "C Assuming that this is just a \"Bad English\" question.",
      "Regardless of reason.\nThe worst is when you have to clear individual users from any folder or file left by previous employee.\n\nBest practice is always to place user in group",
      "I just can not understand CompTIA logic to select this ansvers so only option is B.\nC is not correct because word trap (specific in question, all in C ansver)",
      "IT's C..\nclear for me",
      "Best answer that meet objective is B:\nIt is NOT a best practice to assign permission to individual user BUT it is BEST answer among others",
      "Sh*t I just changed my mind, yes I am going for B and I hate even possibility that is something that CompTIA think it is A OK!",
      "Only Sh*t I see here from CompTIA is that thez put vague questions and even more vague ansvers, in question is SPECIFIC users in least wrong ansver there is word ALL users.\nWhen I remember I was cursing CISCO for trick questions, now when my MCSA needs replacement I miss normal trick questions.",
      "I disagree. Giving explicit rights is just opening pandora's box of troubles if you have many users on domain. I prefer to make group even there is only one current user on some FS folder. That way you know every access right directly from AD and easy to manage when it comes to position or function change.\nSo I go with C",
      "How are the terms \"all users\" and \"specific users\" synonymous. None of the answers shown are correct."
    ],
