  },
  {
    "exam_code": "sk0-005",
    "topic": 1,
    "question_num": 114,
    "question": "Which of the following ensures a secondary network path is available if the primary connection fails?",
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "Link aggregation",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "Most recently used",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "Heartbeat",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "Fault tolerance",
        "is_correct": true
      }
    ],
    "suggested_answers": [
      "D"
    ],
    "answers": [
      "Link Aggregation combines multiple physical ports into one logical port that does provide redundancy, however the question states \"secondary network path\". I'm going with D",
      "Link aggregation also ensures a secondary network path is available if the primary connection fails. Both Link aggregation and Fault tolerance are valid answers, but in the context of specifically ensuring a secondary network path, Link aggregation is the more precise term.",
      "The purpose of link aggregation is to increase bandwidth AND provide fault tolerance.",
      "link agg. includes failover automatically",
      "for sure D:",
      "Agree with FineB",
      "The D is correct. Option A will only Aggregate the link for better performance like bandwidth."
    ],
    "url": "https://www.examtopics.com/discussions/comptia/view/106835-exam-sk0-005-topic-1-question-114-discussion/"
  },
  {
    "exam_code": "sk0-005",
    "topic": 1,
    "question_num": 115,
    "question": "A developer is creating a web application that will contain five web nodes. The developer's main goal is to ensure the application is always available to the end users. Which of the following should the developer use when designing the web application?",
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "Round robin",
        "is_correct": true
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "Link aggregation",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "Network address translation",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "Bridged networking",
        "is_correct": false
      }
    ],
    "suggested_answers": [
      "A"
    ],
    "answers": [
      "A. Round robin.\n\nTo ensure high availability and distribute the load across multiple web nodes, the developer should use a load balancing technique such as round-robin. With round-robin, incoming requests are distributed evenly across the available web nodes in a circular order, which helps to prevent any one node from becoming overwhelmed with traffic.\n\nLink aggregation, network address translation, and bridged networking are all networking technologies that can be used in various contexts, but they are not specifically designed to ensure high availability of web applications."
    ],
    "url": "https://www.examtopics.com/discussions/comptia/view/105401-exam-sk0-005-topic-1-question-115-discussion/"
  },
  {
    "exam_code": "sk0-005",
    "topic": 1,
    "question_num": 116,
    "question": "Which of the following is the MOST secure method to access servers located in remote branch offices?",
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "Use an MFA out-of-band solution",
        "is_correct": true
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "Use a Telnet connection",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "Use a password complexity policy",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "Use a role-based access policy",
        "is_correct": false
      }
    ],
    "suggested_answers": [
      "A"
    ],
    "answers": [
      "A. Use an MFA out-of-band solution.\n\nThe most secure method to access servers located in remote branch offices is to use an MFA (multi-factor authentication) out-of-band solution. MFA adds an extra layer of security beyond the traditional username and password by requiring an additional form of authentication, such as a fingerprint or a one-time code sent to a mobile device. An out-of-band solution means that the second factor is sent through a separate channel or device, further reducing the risk of compromise.\n\nTelnet connections are not secure because they transmit data in clear text, making it vulnerable to interception and unauthorized access. Password complexity policies and role-based access policies are important security measures, but they alone are not enough to provide sufficient security for remote access to servers.",
      "I think answer is A",
      "I think answer is A\nMFA for the entire network and also to access a remote server.\nSome examples of out-of-band authentication (when paired with a typical online login) include:\nPush notifications sent to a mobile device\nQR codes with encrypted transaction data\nBiometric readers for fingerprint scans or facial recognition\nPhone calls for voice authentication",
      "MFA and out of band could be an answer but MFA isn't specific to just secure remote access .. it is for the entire network. Out of band would be authentication that is done outside of the original communication. \nTelnet is not secure\nA password complexity policy has nothing to do with most secure access method\n\nRBAC provides a consistent authentication and authorization mechanism for users access. Role-based access control (RBAC) restricts network access based on a person's role within an organization and has become one of the main methods for advanced access control. The roles in RBAC refer to the levels of access that employees have to the network.",
      "A is the correct answer. RBAC without OOB is useless as the server is needed to be accessed remotely"
    ],
    "url": "https://www.examtopics.com/discussions/comptia/view/82571-exam-sk0-005-topic-1-question-116-discussion/"
  },
  {
    "exam_code": "sk0-005",
    "topic": 1,
    "question_num": 117,
    "question": "A server administrator is installing a new server that uses 40Gb network connectivity. The administrator needs to find the proper cables to connect the server to the switch. Which of the following connectors should the administrator use?",
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "SFP+",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "GBIC",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "SFP",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "QSFP+",
        "is_correct": true
      }
    ],
    "suggested_answers": [
      "D"
    ],
    "answers": [
      "D. QSFP+ is the proper connector to use for 40Gb network connectivity. SFP+ and SFP are connectors commonly used for 10Gb network connectivity, while GBIC is an older connector type that is less commonly used today.",
      "D is correct.\n\nSFP = 1Gbps\nSFP+ = 10Gbps\nQSFP+ = 40 Gbps\nQSFP28 = 100Gbps\n\nGBIC (Gigabit Interface Converter)",
      "https://www.3coptics.com/News/28.html"
    ],
    "url": "https://www.examtopics.com/discussions/comptia/view/85968-exam-sk0-005-topic-1-question-117-discussion/"
  },
  {
    "exam_code": "sk0-005",
    "topic": 1,
    "question_num": 118,
    "question": "A technician is laying out a filesystem on a new Linux server. Which of the following tools would work BEST to allow the technician to increase a partition's size in the future without reformatting it?",
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "LVM",
        "is_correct": true
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "DiskPart",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "fdisk",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "Format",
        "is_correct": false
      }
    ],
    "suggested_answers": [
      "A"
    ],
    "answers": [
      "A. LVM\n\nThe Logical Volume Manager (LVM) is a device mapper framework that provides logical volume management for the Linux kernel. LVM allows for flexible disk management, enabling the resizing of partitions (logical volumes) on the fly without the need for unmounting or reformatting them. This capability makes it an ideal choice for systems where disk space requirements may change over time, providing administrators with the ability to increase or decrease the size of partitions as needed without disrupting services.",
      "LVM why creating confusion ?",
      "LVM is the best tool to allow a technician to increase a partition's size in the future without reformatting it. LVM provides flexibility in managing storage by abstracting physical storage devices into logical volumes that can be resized, extended, or shrunk without the need to reformat or repartition the file system. This makes it an ideal choice for dynamically managing storage on a Linux server.",
      "A. LVM\nLVM allows a Logical Volume to span multiple physical disks/RAID sets. If you run out of space on your current disk, just add a new disk to the system, use it to extend the volume group that needs more space, and add the necessary space to your logical volume. If you need to move your data to new disks and remove the old ones, you can use pvmove or LVM-level mirroring to migrate the data to the new disks. All this can be done while filesystems are mounted and applications are running.\n\nWith FDISK, you can extend an existing partition only if there is free space on the disk immediately after the partition. So extending any partitions other than the last one on the disk will require moving partitions around, which cannot be done while the partition is mounted.",
      "Diskpart to extend or resize without formatting",
      "Isn't this C?"
    ],
    "url": "https://www.examtopics.com/discussions/comptia/view/106521-exam-sk0-005-topic-1-question-118-discussion/"
  },
  {
    "exam_code": "sk0-005",
    "topic": 1,
    "question_num": 119,
    "question": "Which of the following should be placed at the top of a Bash script to ensure it can be executed?",
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "bash",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "!execute",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "#!",
        "is_correct": true
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "@echo off",
        "is_correct": false
      }
    ],
    "suggested_answers": [
      "C"
    ],
    "answers": [
      "https://earthly.dev/blog/understanding-bash/"
    ],
    "url": "https://www.examtopics.com/discussions/comptia/view/85969-exam-sk0-005-topic-1-question-119-discussion/"
  },
  {
    "exam_code": "sk0-005",
    "topic": 1,
    "question_num": 120,
    "question": "A systems administrator has several different types of hard drives. The administrator is setting up a NAS that will allow end users to see all the drives within the\nNAS. Which of the following storage types should the administrator use?",
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "RAID array",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "Serial Attached SCSI",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "Solid-state drive",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "Just a bunch of disks",
        "is_correct": true
      }
    ],
    "suggested_answers": [
      "D"
    ],
    "answers": [
      "D. Just a bunch of disks (JBOD)\n\nJust a Bunch Of Disks (JBOD) is a storage architecture that groups multiple hard drives together, allowing each drive to be seen and accessed independently by the operating system or users. This setup does not provide redundancy or performance enhancements like RAID configurations but does enable the use of drives of different sizes and types within the same enclosure or NAS system.",
      "Because of the varying sizes/types of HDs in the question and that is requests end users to see ALL the drives within JBOD would be a better selection than RAID. With a RAID it would appear as one logical unit and the varying sizes would have a limit of the smaller disks used.\n\nhttps://www.techtarget.com/searchstorage/definition/JBOD"
    ],
    "url": "https://www.examtopics.com/discussions/comptia/view/82573-exam-sk0-005-topic-1-question-120-discussion/"
  },
  {
    "exam_code": "sk0-005",
    "topic": 1,
    "question_num": 121,
    "question": "Which of the following is the BEST action to perform before applying patches to one of the hosts in a high availability cluster?",
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "Disable the heartbeat network.",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "Fallback cluster services.",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "Set the cluster to active-active.",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "Failover all VMs.",
        "is_correct": true
      }
    ],
    "suggested_answers": [
      "D"
    ],
    "answers": [
      "D. This ensure a noted is actively running while the patch is being applied to another.",
      "D. Failover all VMs\n\nThis ensures that the VMs remain operational and accessible while the host system is being updated, minimizing downtime and maintaining the availability of services.\n\nWhy?\nMaintains Service Availability: By failing over VMs to another host, critical services and applications continue to run without interruption.\nMinimizes Risk: Moving VMs away from the host being patched reduces the risk of service disruption if unexpected issues arise during the patching process.\nFailing over all VMs to another host within the cluster is a direct and effective way to ensure that services remain available and uninterrupted during the maintenance process, adhering to best practices for managing high availability environments.",
      "D. Failover all VMs. This option means to move all the virtual machines running on the host that needs to be patched to another host in the cluster. This way, the host can be safely patched without affecting the availability or performance of the virtual machines. Failover can be done manually or automatically by using a cluster management tool or a hypervisor feature.",
      "Falling back cluster services is not the BEST action to perform before applying patches to one of the hosts in a high availability cluster because it does not ensure that there is no downtime during the patching process. Failing over all VMs ensures that there is no downtime during the patching process.",
      "I believe C is the answer. The purpose of Active-Active is to have zero down-time. If a node fails, it automatically sends all that traffic to the other running node (as they were active-active, aka \"Live Failover\"). This would allow the patching/rebooting to occur with no downtime. Also, it is less work than failing over everything manually etc, and it does state \"BEST\" action to perform.",
      "Cluster mode (active-active or active-passive) is a design-time configuration, not a maintenance procedure. Changing it before patching doesn’t make sense and could introduce instability.\nI'd go with D, too.",
      "Cluster Patching Process\nThe process to patch cluster nodes is to bring down one node at a time for patching (and the subsequent reboot) and then return it to service. This process is accomplished for each cluster member, never bringing down all of the nodes simultaneously. It is not usually realistic to manage this process manually. Services such as Microsoft SCCM and Microsoft Cluster Aware Updating can automate the process.\nThe Official CompTIA Server+ Study Guide (Exam SK0-005)\n\nI think the most important is the process to patch cluster nodes is to bring down one node at a time for patching (and the subsequent reboot) and then return it to service.\n\nIf you have one (or more) VMs on active cluster server, you have to failover the VM(s) to patch the active cluster server.",
      "Failover and Failback\nWhen you research server clustering, you frequently hear the terms failover and failback. Failover refers to the failure of the active service provider node and passive node's taking over of its responsibilities. At that point, the server that was the passive node becomes the active node. It services clients and handles the cluster’s duties. Once the original active node returns to service, you may set it to act as the new passive node, or you may shift the services back to it, causing it to take back its active node role and relegating the formerly passive server to its original role. The term failback refers to the service returning to the original active node.\nThe Official CompTIA Server+ Study Guide (Exam SK0-005) page 242",
      "I think the \"B. Fallback cluster services.\" is an incorrect spell. Even if they spell correctly it's not the best answer.\n\nIf choice B is \"Failover cluster services.\" it will be the best answer to this question.",
      "I also think the answer is D. If you have several vm's on your cluster of servers, you could have; for example, vm1 and vm2 connected to cluster server 1, vm3 connected to cluster server 2 and so on. It makes sense to Failover all VM's located on cluster server 1 to another cluster server prior to applying patches to that server. Just my thoughts.",
      "I think the answer is D",
      "According to compTIA, Active-Active provides high Availability and load Balancing\nActive-Passive provides only high Availability, so the HA cluster mentioned in the question can be Active-Active or Active-Passive, so C is not correct.\nFallback = Role back = alternative which can be used if something goes wrong with the main plan and this is not making sense in this question, so B is not correct.\nI think the answer is D.",
      "Secondary servers are already active and waiting to receive connections, so there is no downtime during failover scenario. The environment enjoys improved the processing capacity because two server nodes are actively running instead of one waiting Idly as it does in Active/Passive Clustering.\n\nTherefore you can remove a single instance from the cluster to patch the server and this would be transparent to the users. Failovers may not be.\n\nhttps://www.sanitysolutions.com/active-clustering-101/#",
      "B\nreference comptia official server + SK0-005\nThe purpose behind server clusters is to provide high availability of services and\ndata, scalability, and load balancing.\nConsider the following example: You have a web site hosting critical customer data.\nA BIA determined that if the website becomes unavailable, there will be severe\nconsequences for the organization. As the sysadmin, you decide to create a two-node\ncluster of web servers. The two servers each host duplicate copies of the files that\nmake up the web site. If one server fails, the other server is configured to automatically\nand immediately take over. Since both nodes share the same IP address, there is no\ninterruption or reconfiguration necessary. Also, if you need to take the servers down\nfor maintenance, you can do so one at a time, allowing the site to remain available\nduring patching or upgrade procedures",
      "Two issues with this answer. First it states Fallback and not Failback. Second a failback has to do with the primary server becoming unavailable and then failing back when it is back online. The term here would be failover for what I believe that you are thinking.",
      "I prefer option C"
    ],
    "url": "https://www.examtopics.com/discussions/comptia/view/82362-exam-sk0-005-topic-1-question-121-discussion/"
  },
  {
    "exam_code": "sk0-005",
    "topic": 1,
    "question_num": 122,
    "question": "A server administrator is deploying a new server that has two hard drives on which to install the OS. Which of the following RAID configurations should be used to provide redundancy for the OS?",
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "RAID 0",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "RAID 1",
        "is_correct": true
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "RAID 5",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "RAID 6",
        "is_correct": false
      }
    ],
    "suggested_answers": [
      "B"
    ],
    "answers": [
      "B - RAID 1",
      "RAID 1 Disk Mirroring\nThis RAID design provides a very high degree of redundancy. All content is written to both disks, and if one disk fails, the other disk has everything. This solution is excellent for mission-critical situations. The downside to mirroring is that drive space is not very efficiently used (if you buy two disks that are 1 TB each, you’ve paid for 2 TB of storage but will only have the effective capacity of 1 TB). RAID 1 requires a minimum of two disks.\n\nThe Official CompTIA Server+ Study Guide (Exam SK0-005) page 114.",
      "RAID 1 would be mirroring. RAID 0 striping and therefore no redundancy.\n\nRAIDs 5 & 6 require a minimum 3/4 disks so cannot be the answer here.\n\nhttps://www.prepressure.com/library/technology/raid"
    ],
    "url": "https://www.examtopics.com/discussions/comptia/view/82574-exam-sk0-005-topic-1-question-122-discussion/"
  },
  {
    "exam_code": "sk0-005",
    "topic": 1,
    "question_num": 123,
    "question": "A systems administrator has been alerted to a zero-day vulnerability that is impacting a service enabled on a server OS. Which of the following would work BEST to limit an attacker from exploiting this vulnerability?",
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "Installing the latest patches",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "Closing open ports",
        "is_correct": true
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "Enabling antivirus protection",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "Enabling a NIDS",
        "is_correct": false
      }
    ],
    "suggested_answers": [
      "B"
    ],
    "answers": [
      "B - Closing Open Ports",
      "Correct Answer: B. Closing open ports\n\nIncorrect answer: NIDS - because the \"D\" stands for detection, not PREVENTION. NIDS would not stop an attack it would only generate an alert.",
      "- Nothing in the question is asking to detect the zero day attack, so NIDS is not relevant. We already know about the attack.\n- Nothing in the question is asking us to keep the service running with our action.\n- There is a low probability that a patch will solve an issue of a zero day attack.\n- Closing the port is the only option that can prevent malware traffic effecting the particular service.",
      "B. Closing open ports. This option means blocking or disabling the network ports used by the service affected by the zero-day vulnerability. Closing open ports can work best to limit an attacker from exploiting the zero-day vulnerability, as it can prevent or reduce the exposure of the service to the network and reduce the attack surface. Closing open ports can also help to isolate the host from potential attacks and minimize the impact on other hosts or systems in the cluster.",
      "assumption: a zero-day vulnerability means the patch is still not available, so answer is D: Enabling a NIDS",
      "Since we know the services with zero-day vulnerabilities, I think closing ports is better than installing NIDS. so B is a better answer",
      "Depending on business needs, closing a port may not be a viable option. NIDS feels like the most likely answer to be correct.",
      "But is not NIDS a \"detection\" system? The questions asks what is best to limit an attacker from exploiting the vulnerability, whereas a NIDS will simply tell you once it happened. In this case, answer B would be it (Assuming they are closing the specific ports utilized in the zero-day). A&C obviously are no good against zero-day."
    ],
    "url": "https://www.examtopics.com/discussions/comptia/view/85974-exam-sk0-005-topic-1-question-123-discussion/"
  },
  {
    "exam_code": "sk0-005",
    "topic": 1,
    "question_num": 124,
    "question": "A technician is checking a server rack. Upon entering the room, the technician notices the fans on a particular server in the rack are running at high speeds. This is the only server in the rack that is experiencing this behavior. The ambient temperature in the room appears to be normal. Which of the following is the MOST likely reason why the fans in that server are operating at full speed?",
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "The server is in the process of shutting down, so fan speed operations have been defaulted to high.",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "An incorrect fan size was inserted into the server, and the server has had to increase the fan speed to compensate.",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "A fan failure has occurred, and the other fans have increased speed to compensate.",
        "is_correct": true
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "The server is utilizing more memory than the other servers, so it has increased the fans to compensate.",
        "is_correct": false
      }
    ],
    "suggested_answers": [
      "C"
    ],
    "answers": [
      "C. A fan failure is the MOST likely reason why the fans in that server are operating at full speed while the other servers in the rack are operating normally. When a fan fails, the other fans in the server have to work harder to compensate for the loss of airflow. This increased workload causes the remaining fans to run at a higher speed than normal to maintain adequate cooling for the server components.\n\nOption A is incorrect because the fan speed is not dependent on the server shutting down. Option B is unlikely because the server would not be designed to increase the fan speed to compensate for an incorrect fan size. Option D is also unlikely because fan speed is not dependent on the memory usage of the server."
    ],
    "url": "https://www.examtopics.com/discussions/comptia/view/105452-exam-sk0-005-topic-1-question-124-discussion/"
  },
  {
    "exam_code": "sk0-005",
    "topic": 1,
    "question_num": 125,
    "question": "A server administrator is installing an OS on a new server. Company policy states no one is to log in directly to the server. Which of the following installation methods is BEST suited to meet the company policy?",
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "GUI",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "Core",
        "is_correct": true
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "Virtualized",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "Clone",
        "is_correct": false
      }
    ],
    "suggested_answers": [
      "B"
    ],
    "answers": [
      "Shouldn't this be vitualized",
      "I agree, if you install Core onto a new server, you can still log into it via console",
      "B - Core is the best solution",
      "This is what I found. A core installation, also known as a headless or minimal installation, provides a command-line interface without a graphical user interface (GUI). This approach aligns with the company policy for several reasons:\nReduced attack surface: A core installation eliminates unnecessary components and services, minimizing potential vulnerabilities.\nRemote management: Core installations are typically managed remotely through secure protocols like SSH, eliminating the need for direct local logins.\nEfficiency: Core installations use fewer system resources, allowing the server to dedicate more power to its primary functions.\nCompliance: By not having a GUI, it discourages direct logins and encourages proper remote management practices, adhering to the company policy.",
      "A. GUI (Graphical User Interface): Installing the OS through a graphical interface typically requires physically connecting a monitor, keyboard, and mouse to the server. This would involve direct login access, potentially violating the company policy.\nB. Core: While a core installation might reduce the server's attack surface compared to a full GUI installation, it wouldn't eliminate the need for direct login for configuration.\nD. Clone: Cloning an existing server creates a copy of the existing system configuration. While potentially faster for initial setup, it wouldn't address the core policy of avoiding direct login to the new server, especially if the cloned server contains unnecessary accounts or security vulnerabilities.",
      "Chat GPT said this\nB. Core installation method is best suited to meet the company policy.\n\nA core installation method installs only the essential components required to run the operating system. It does not include a graphical user interface (GUI) or any other extra features that are not required for the server's function. This minimal installation reduces the attack surface of the server, making it more secure.\n\nSince company policy states that no one is to log in directly to the server, a core installation is the best choice as it removes any unnecessary components and services that could be exploited by unauthorized users.\n\nVirtualized installation and clone installation are not relevant to the company policy of prohibiting direct logins. A GUI installation includes unnecessary features and services that could pose security risks. Therefore, a core installation is the best option for meeting the company policy.",
      "Bbbbbbbbb"
    ],
    "url": "https://www.examtopics.com/discussions/comptia/view/87327-exam-sk0-005-topic-1-question-125-discussion/"
  },
  {
    "exam_code": "sk0-005",
    "topic": 1,
    "question_num": 126,
    "question": "Which of the following licenses would MOST likely include vendor assistance?",
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "Open-source",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "Version compatibility",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "Subscription",
        "is_correct": true
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "Maintenance and support",
        "is_correct": false
      }
    ],
    "suggested_answers": [
      "C"
    ],
    "answers": [
      "ChatGPT:\nC. Subscription ⚠️ (Partially correct)\n\nA subscription license (like Microsoft 365 or Red Hat Enterprise Linux) often includes updates and limited support, as long as the subscription is active.\n\nHowever, not all subscription licenses guarantee vendor assistance — some are “updates only.” So while plausible, it’s not guaranteed.\n\nD. Maintenance and support ✅\nA maintenance and support license (or agreement) explicitly includes:\nVendor assistance (technical support, troubleshooting)\nSoftware updates and patches\nAccess to knowledge bases or priority helpdesk services\n\nThis is the clearest and most direct type of license that ensures vendor help is available.\n✅ Best answer.\n\nAnd I leaned that way too, while choosing an answer.",
      "Subcriptions normally applies to software while maintenance is normally applied to hardward. Additionally, subscription is a type of license while maintenance is an agreement/contract. I hope this helps.",
      "Wording. Maintenance and Support contracts don't normally support server software, but server hardware.",
      "Maintenance and Support Plans\nIt is important not to confuse the licensing and maintenance concepts. Just because you have the legal right to use a piece of software or install an OS does not necessarily entitle you to vendor support for the product. Some licensing models may include support. Subscription license models are an example. Support plans are covered later in the book.\n\nThe Official CompTIA Server+ Study Guide (Exam SK0-005) page 22.",
      "I agree. I have never heard of a \"maintenance and support\" license.",
      "Subscription - is type of license that can be supported and maintained by vendor",
      "Subscription - Maintenance and support are normally a part of a license and not listed as a license type itself - Neither is version compatability. Open-source is free and doesn't normally offer any kind of support except communities."
    ],
    "url": "https://www.examtopics.com/discussions/comptia/view/82576-exam-sk0-005-topic-1-question-126-discussion/"
  },
  {
    "exam_code": "sk0-005",
    "topic": 1,
    "question_num": 127,
    "question": "A company wants to deploy software to all users, but very few of them will be using the software at any one point in time. Which of the following licensing models would be BEST for the company?",
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "Per site",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "Per concurrent user",
        "is_correct": true
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "Per core",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "Per instance",
        "is_correct": false
      }
    ],
    "suggested_answers": [
      "B"
    ],
    "answers": [
      "Per-concurrent-user—one license for each software instance in use by a user. This is typically less expensive than per-seat licensing. If your organization has ten of these licenses, and there are twenty copies of the software installed, then only ten users may use the software simultaneously.\n\nThe Official CompTIA Server+ Study Guide (Exam SK0-005) page 23."
    ],
    "url": "https://www.examtopics.com/discussions/comptia/view/87237-exam-sk0-005-topic-1-question-127-discussion/"
  },
  {
    "exam_code": "sk0-005",
    "topic": 1,
    "question_num": 128,
    "question": "A technician is setting up a physical server that will be used to store sensitive data. The technician plans to set up the internal hard drives in a RAID 5 array for redundancy. Which of the following would be the BEST type of interface for the technician to use?",
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "SAS",
        "is_correct": true
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "USB",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "SD",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "eSATA",
        "is_correct": false
      }
    ],
    "suggested_answers": [
      "A"
    ],
    "answers": [
      "A. SAS (Serial Attached SCSI) would be the BEST type of interface for the technician to use for setting up a RAID 5 array for storing sensitive data on a physical server. SAS provides higher performance, reliability, and data transfer rates compared to other interface types such as USB, SD, or eSATA. Additionally, SAS has advanced features like dual-porting, which provides redundancy and multipathing capabilities for improved reliability and availability of data. Therefore, SAS is a popular choice for enterprise-level storage applications that require high-performance and reliable storage solutions."
    ],
    "url": "https://www.examtopics.com/discussions/comptia/view/105451-exam-sk0-005-topic-1-question-128-discussion/"
  },
  {
    "exam_code": "sk0-005",
    "topic": 1,
    "question_num": 129,
    "question": "A technician is working on a Linux server. The customer has reported that files in the home directory are missing. The /etc/fstab file has the following entry: nfsserver:/home /home nfs defaults 0 0\nHowever, a df -h /home command returns the following information:\n/dev/sda2 10G 1G 9G 10% /home\nWhich of the following should the technician attempt FIRST to resolve the issue?",
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "mkdir /home",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "umount nfsserver:/home",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "rmdir nfsserver:/home/dev/sda2",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "mount /home",
        "is_correct": true
      }
    ],
    "suggested_answers": [
      "D"
    ],
    "answers": [
      "“A. mkdir /home” - This is unnecessary and has no effect because the /home directory already exists and the local partition is mounted.\n\n“B. umount nfsserver:/home” - This command will result in an error because the NFS mount has not been done.\n\n“C. rmdir nfsserver:/home/dev/sda2” - This is an incorrect path specification and will cause an error when executed. Also, it is dangerous to attempt to remove a directory on a remote server.\n\nTherefore, executing “D. mount /home” is the first and most appropriate step to solve this problem. This will hopefully result in a correct NFS mount based on the settings in /etc/fstab and make the user's files available again.",
      "The mount /home command attempts to mount /home based on the existing entry in /etc/fstab. This would attempt to mount nfsserver:/home to /home as specified. Since the issue seems to be that the NFS share did not mount properly, this is the correct first step to attempt to resolve the issue.",
      "B. Mount the NFS share using the mount command. This option means using the command-line tool to connect the remote NFS share to the local mount point. Mounting the NFS share can resolve the issue, as it can restore access to the files in the home directory that are stored on the NFS server. Mounting the NFS share can also verify that the NFS client package is installed and configured correctly on the Linux server.",
      "Ɓbbbbbbbbbb",
      "D: is the first thing to try. A correct fstab entry and /home folder exists..",
      "The fstab (/etc/fstab) (or file systems table) file is a system configuration file on Debian systems. The fstab file typically lists all available disks and disk partitions, and indicates how they are to be initialized or otherwise integrated into the overall system's file system.\n\nThe etc/fstab file has /home as the DIR.. but when a df /home is asked for there is no /home directory underneath... only /dev/sda2. Therefore, we would need to mkdir a /home under the /home directory.\n\n# <file system>     <dir>       <type>   <options>   <dump>    <pass>\n10.10.0.10:/backups /var/backups  nfs      defaults    0       0\n\nhttps://linuxize.com/post/how-to-mount-an-nfs-share-in-linux/",
      "Run the mount command in one of the following forms to mount the NFS share:\n\nmount /var/backups\nmount 10.10.0.10:/backups\nCopy\nThe mount command, will read the content of the /etc/fstab and mount the share.\n\nNext time you reboot the system the NFS share will be mounted automatically.\n\nhttps://linuxize.com/post/how-to-mount-an-nfs-share-in-linux/",
      "A is wrong as /home exists! if you use df -h /blah and the folder does not exists, you get a \"directory not found\" reply"
    ],
    "url": "https://www.examtopics.com/discussions/comptia/view/82580-exam-sk0-005-topic-1-question-129-discussion/"
  },
  {
    "exam_code": "sk0-005",
    "topic": 1,
    "question_num": 130,
    "question": "A company is reviewing options for its current disaster recovery plan and potential changes to it. The security team will not allow customer data to egress to non- company equipment, and the company has requested recovery in the shortest possible time. Which of the following will BEST meet these goals?",
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "A warm site",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "A hot site",
        "is_correct": true
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "Cloud recovery",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "A cold site",
        "is_correct": false
      }
    ],
    "suggested_answers": [
      "B"
    ],
    "answers": [
      "B. Duh",
      "Given the constraints mentioned:\n\nCustomer data should not egress to non-company equipment.\nRecovery in the shortest possible time.\nThe best option would be:\n\nB. A hot site\n\n\nGiven the requirements, a hot site is the best choice because it meets the need for rapid recovery and can be owned by the company, ensuring customer data does not egress to non-company equipment.",
      "Bbbbbbbbbb",
      "Answer is B. Companies may choose to build/maintain their own hot site. And the questions asks for recovery in the shortest possible time.",
      "B: is correct. It's a replication of the primary DC in all aspects with data replication. There is no rule, that the equipment must belong to another company (see comment of Timock2)",
      "What if the company is Amazon or Microsoft? Given the AWS and MS Azure services they provide, it seems as though they actually own their hot sites.",
      "A warm site does not have the data and would fit with the no data on a NON-company equipment requirement. \n\nA hot side and cloud recovery would have non-company equipment and the cold site is not the shortest possible time."
    ],
    "url": "https://www.examtopics.com/discussions/comptia/view/82582-exam-sk0-005-topic-1-question-130-discussion/"
  },
  {
    "exam_code": "sk0-005",
    "topic": 1,
    "question_num": 131,
    "question": "Which of the following encryption methodologies would MOST likely be used to ensure encrypted data cannot be retrieved if a device is stolen?",
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "End-to-end encryption",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "Encryption in transit",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "Encryption at rest",
        "is_correct": true
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "Public key encryption",
        "is_correct": false
      }
    ],
    "suggested_answers": [
      "C"
    ],
    "answers": [
      "C. Encryption at rest\n\nEncryption at rest is specifically designed to protect data stored on devices such as hard drives, USB drives, or mobile devices. When data is encrypted at rest, it remains encrypted even if the device is stolen or compromised. This ensures that the data cannot be accessed without the appropriate decryption keys, thereby safeguarding it from unauthorized access.",
      "C. Encryption at rest\n\nEncryption at rest is a method used to encrypt data stored on a device or media, such as hard drives, ensuring that even if the device is stolen, the data remains secure. This encryption protects the data when it is not actively being used, making it a suitable choice for scenarios where the primary concern is securing data on storage devices.",
      "C. Encryption at rest\n\nEncryption at rest is specifically designed to protect data stored on devices such as laptops, mobile devices, and servers. If a device is stolen, encryption at rest ensures that the data remains encrypted and inaccessible to unauthorized individuals. This type of encryption is applied to the storage media itself, making it difficult for someone to retrieve sensitive information even if they have physical possession of the device.",
      "C. Encryption at rest\nEncrypt it while you still have control. Once it's stolen, it's unusable.",
      "answer is B"
    ],
    "url": "https://www.examtopics.com/discussions/comptia/view/123337-exam-sk0-005-topic-1-question-131-discussion/"
  },
  {
    "exam_code": "sk0-005",
    "topic": 1,
    "question_num": 132,
    "question": "A company stores extremely sensitive data on an air-gapped system. Which of the following can be implemented to increase security against a potential insider threat?",
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "Two-person integrity",
        "is_correct": true
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "SSO",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "SIEM",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "Faraday cage",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "E",
        "text": "MFA",
        "is_correct": false
      }
    ],
    "suggested_answers": [
      "A"
    ],
    "answers": [
      "An air gap, air wall, air gapping[1] or disconnected network is a network security measure employed on one or more computers to ensure that a secure computer network is physically isolated from unsecured networks, such as the public Internet or an unsecured local area network.[2] It means a computer or network has no network interface controllers connected to other networks,[3][4] with a physical or conceptual air gap, analogous to the air gap used in plumbing to maintain water quality.",
      "https://csrc.nist.gov/glossary/term/two_person_integrity"
    ],
    "url": "https://www.examtopics.com/discussions/comptia/view/85976-exam-sk0-005-topic-1-question-132-discussion/"
  },
  {
    "exam_code": "sk0-005",
    "topic": 1,
    "question_num": 133,
    "question": "Which of the following can be used to map a network drive to a user profile?",
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "System service",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "Network service",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "Login script",
        "is_correct": true
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "Kickstart script",
        "is_correct": false
      }
    ],
    "suggested_answers": [
      "C"
    ],
    "answers": [
      "A login script is a script that is executed when a user logs into a computer. A login script can adjust settings in the operating system, map network drives for different groups of users, or even display a welcome message that is specific to each user. Multiple login scripts can even be utilized at the same time, with specific ones activated based on which user logs in and the operating system in use on the computer.\n\nhttps://www.computerhope.com/jargon/l/login-script.htm",
      "Login and Logout Scripts\n\nSome scripts execute based on the user. For example, a login script runs when a user logs in. The script customizes the user’s environment, maps network drives, and automatically launches a particular application. Logout scripts execute when the user signs off. These scripts might delete temp files or copy the user’s data to a network server.\n\nThe Official CompTIA Server+ Study Guide (Exam SK0-005) page 160."
    ],
    "url": "https://www.examtopics.com/discussions/comptia/view/87275-exam-sk0-005-topic-1-question-133-discussion/"
  },
  {
    "exam_code": "sk0-005",
    "topic": 1,
    "question_num": 134,
    "question": "A server shut down after an extended power outage. When power was restored, the system failed to start. A few seconds into booting, the Num Lock, Scroll Lock, and Caps Lock LEDs flashed several times, and the system stopped. Which of the following is the MOST likely cause of the issue?",
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "The keyboard is defective and needs to be replaced.",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "The system failed before the display card initialized.",
        "is_correct": true
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "The power supply is faulty and is shutting down the system.",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "The NIC has failed, and the system cannot make a network connection.",
        "is_correct": false
      }
    ],
    "suggested_answers": [
      "B"
    ],
    "answers": [
      "✅ C. The power supply is faulty and is shutting down the system.\n\nHere's why:\nThe flashing of the Num Lock, Scroll Lock, and Caps Lock LEDs is a classic POST error code (Power-On Self-Test). This often indicates a hardware-level failure, and when it happens early during boot, it's usually related to critical system components like the power supply, motherboard, or memory.",
      "A bad PSU could prevent boot, but typically the system wouldn’t even start the POST to blink LEDs.\nI'll go with B.",
      "C. I'll go with C becuase it state there was a power outage before the other information was presented about the keyboard lights. Which is pointing to PSU. There could have been a power spike that could affect the PSU.",
      "How do we know it's the video card or display card? It doesn't specifically state if we're able to see the POST/BIOS screen.. not enough information it could be either B or C. The lights on the keyboard flashing does not indicate its a video card issue to me. It could be any component like motherboard, CPU, RAM as surfuganda stated. PSU being faulty, under load and tripping makes more sense to me in this scenario.",
      "B. The system failed before the display card initialized.\n\nExplanation:\nWhen a system fails to boot and the Num Lock, Scroll Lock, and Caps Lock LEDs flash, it usually indicates a hardware failure. However, the fact that the failure occurs before the display card initializes suggests that the issue lies deeper in the system, likely with core hardware components such as the CPU, motherboard, or RAM",
      "Chatgbt agrees with C:\n\"A problematic Power Supply Unit is more likely to cause the described behavior. An issue with the PSU could lead to unstable power delivery, which may trigger the server's built-in protection mechanisms, causing it to shut down. Faulty power supply units are known to cause erratic behavior, including unusual light patterns on the indicator LEDs.\"",
      "The MOST likely cause of the issue is that the system failed before the display card initialized. The flashing of the Num Lock, Scroll Lock, and Caps Lock LEDs is a BIOS error code indicating that the system has failed before the display card is initialized."
    ],
    "url": "https://www.examtopics.com/discussions/comptia/view/114070-exam-sk0-005-topic-1-question-134-discussion/"
  },
  {
    "exam_code": "sk0-005",
    "topic": 1,
    "question_num": 135,
    "question": "A systems administrator is investigating a server with a RAID array that will not boot into the OS. The administrator notices all the hard drives are reporting to be offline. The administrator checks the RAID controller and verifies the configuration is correct. The administrator then replaces one of the drives with a known-good drive, but it appears to be unavailable as well. Next, the administrator takes a drive out of the server and places it in a spare server, and the drive is available and functional. Which of the following is MOST likely causing the issue?",
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "The kernel is corrupt.",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "Resources are misallocated.",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "The backplane has failed.",
        "is_correct": true
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "The drives need to be reseated.",
        "is_correct": false
      }
    ],
    "suggested_answers": [
      "C"
    ],
    "answers": [
      "There may be many causes of storage problems on a server or its related components.\nBoot errors might cause by:\nMisconfigured RAID array\nDrive failure\nController or HBA failure\nLoose connections or cable failures\nCorrupt boot sector\nCorrupt file system table\nBackplane failure\n\nThe server's backplane provides a communications bus among devices that you may install. The backplane is an additional circuit board attached to the motherboard, and not all servers have one. Damage to the backplane—either physical or electrical—may keep devices on this bus from communicating correctly.\n\nThe Official CompTIA Server+ Study Guide (Exam SK0-005) page 122.",
      "C. The backplane has failed.",
      "A looks correct answer"
    ],
    "url": "https://www.examtopics.com/discussions/comptia/view/86285-exam-sk0-005-topic-1-question-135-discussion/"
  },
  {
    "exam_code": "sk0-005",
    "topic": 1,
    "question_num": 136,
    "question": "Which of the following techniques can be configured on a server for network redundancy?",
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "Clustering",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "Virtualizing",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "Cloning",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "Teaming",
        "is_correct": true
      }
    ],
    "suggested_answers": [
      "D"
    ],
    "answers": [
      "Key work network redundancy.\n\nTeaming is about aggregating network connections for better performance and resilience.\nClustering is about grouping servers for high availability and capacity.",
      "If I'm running two firewalls as a cluster in active-passive wouldn't this be considered redundancy for the network?",
      "D. Teaming\n\nNetwork teaming, also known as NIC teaming or bonding, involves grouping multiple network interfaces together to form a single, logical network interface. This configuration provides redundancy and load balancing, enhancing network reliability. If one network interface fails, the traffic is automatically routed through the remaining interfaces, ensuring continued network connectivity. Teaming can be configured to operate in various modes, including fault tolerance for redundancy and load balancing for improved performance.",
      "NOT D.\nTeaming (or more loosely, bridging) makes multiple network cards appear as one card. So for example if you have 2 NICs at 1Gb speed, you can team them together to get up to 2Gb of throughput. With teaming, it is very important to make sure that the network hardware you're plugging into supports it."
    ],
    "url": "https://www.examtopics.com/discussions/comptia/view/130437-exam-sk0-005-topic-1-question-136-discussion/"
  },
  {
    "exam_code": "sk0-005",
    "topic": 1,
    "question_num": 137,
    "question": "A server has experienced several component failures. To minimize downtime, the server administrator wants to replace the components while the server is running. Which of the following can MOST likely be swapped out while the server is still running? (Choose two.)",
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "The power supply",
        "is_correct": true
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "The CPU",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "The hard drive",
        "is_correct": true
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "The GPU",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "E",
        "text": "The cache",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "F",
        "text": "The RAM",
        "is_correct": false
      }
    ],
    "suggested_answers": [
      "A",
      "C"
    ],
    "answers": [
      "24/7 server rarely runs on a single power supply and single harddisk",
      "Forgot to vote",
      "A. The power supply\nand\nC. The hard drive\n\nYou are outside your mind if you think Hot-pluggable RAM is MORE LIKELY than Hot-pluggable hard drive.",
      "A and C are the correct Answer",
      "A. The power supply - In many modern servers, power supplies are designed to be hot-swappable, meaning they can be replaced without powering down the server.\n\nF. The RAM - In some servers, RAM can be added or removed without shutting down the server, especially if the server has redundant memory modules and supports memory hot-plugging.",
      "The only issue is that RAM is an internal component and you would have to power down the blade to open and replace the internal RAM. The hard drive Es.2 is accessible externally and cab be hotswapped without the need to power cycle the device.",
      "The components that can MOST likely be swapped out while the server is still running are:\n\nA. The power supply - In many modern servers, power supplies are designed to be hot-swappable, meaning they can be replaced without powering down the server.\n\nF. The RAM - In some servers, RAM can be added or removed without shutting down the server, especially if the server has redundant memory modules and supports memory hot-plugging.\n\nThe other components listed (CPU, hard drive, GPU, and cache) are typically not hot-swappable, and require the server to be powered down before they can be replaced.",
      "Guessing from the age of this comment, probabaly been posted in 2023-2024. Not to gas light you but to educate other people who came and read the comments, please be advised that mostly the servers in datacentres (H-series or G-series) have external harddrives slots (known as Es.2) which are hotswappable"
    ],
    "url": "https://www.examtopics.com/discussions/comptia/view/108299-exam-sk0-005-topic-1-question-137-discussion/"
  },
  {
    "exam_code": "sk0-005",
    "topic": 1,
    "question_num": 138,
    "question": "An administrator is configuring a host-based firewall for a server. The server needs to allow SSH, FTP, and LDAP traffic. Which of the following ports must be configured so this traffic will be allowed? (Choose three.)",
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "21",
        "is_correct": true
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "22",
        "is_correct": true
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "53",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "67",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "E",
        "text": "69",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "F",
        "text": "110",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "G",
        "text": "123",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "H",
        "text": "389",
        "is_correct": true
      }
    ],
    "suggested_answers": [
      "A",
      "B",
      "H"
    ],
    "answers": [
      "A. 21 (FTP)\nB. 22 (SSH)\nH. 389 (LDAP)",
      "A. 21 (FTP)\nB. 22 (SSH)\nH. 389 (LDAP)",
      "B. 22 (SSH)\nA. 21 (FTP)\nH. 389 (LDAP)\n\nPort 53 is used for DNS\nPorts 67 and 69 are used for DHCP\nPort 110 is used for POP3 email retrieval\nPort 123 is used for NTP (network time protocol)",
      "Port 21 - FTP\nPort 22 - SSH\nPort 389 - LDAP"
    ],
    "url": "https://www.examtopics.com/discussions/comptia/view/104227-exam-sk0-005-topic-1-question-138-discussion/"
  },
  {
    "exam_code": "sk0-005",
    "topic": 1,
    "question_num": 139,
    "question": "A technician needs to configure a server's RAID array for maximum capacity. Which of the following RAID levels BEST meets this requirement?",
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "0",
        "is_correct": true
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "1",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "5",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "6",
        "is_correct": false
      }
    ],
    "suggested_answers": [
      "A"
    ],
    "answers": [
      "in 20 years of doing IT work I have never encountered a scenario where RAID 0 was an acceptable solution. Questions like this are why no one respects comptia certs."
    ],
    "url": "https://www.examtopics.com/discussions/comptia/view/143977-exam-sk0-005-topic-1-question-139-discussion/"
  },
  {
    "exam_code": "sk0-005",
    "topic": 1,
    "question_num": 140,
    "question": "A user is unable to modify files on a Windows fileshare. A technician examines the system and determines the user is a member of Group A. Group A was granted full control over the files. Which of the following is MOST likely causing the access issues?",
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "SIEM policies",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "IDS rules",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "Share permissions",
        "is_correct": true
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "Firewall ports",
        "is_correct": false
      }
    ],
    "suggested_answers": [
      "C"
    ],
    "answers": [
      "C. Share permissions\n\nThe most likely cause of the access issue is the share permissions. Even though Group A was granted full control over the files, it's possible that the share permissions are restricting access to the files. Share permissions are used to control access to a shared folder or file across the network, and they can be set independently of the NTFS permissions that control access to the files on the local system.\n\nSIEM policies, IDS rules, and firewall ports are all security measures that could potentially impact access to the files, but they are less likely to be the cause of the access issue in this scenario.",
      "Share Permissions\nWhen a resource is shared over a network, it can be assigned Read, Change, Full Control, or Deny permissions on the share. Windows supports user-level security; users have permissions depending upon who they are. Share permissions only protect the resource from access over the network. Local access can only be secured by NTFS permissions.\nThe Official CompTIA Server+ Study Guide (Exam SK0-005) page 264.\n\nResource Access\nMany help desk tickets and user complaints center around access to resources. Frequently, these issues are tied to permissions. In Windows, file access is managed by NTFS permissions and by Share permissions when accessing the file from across the network. In Linux, file access is controlled by standard permissions and by NFS permissions when accessing the data from across the network. Ensuring these permissions are set correctly eases many user frustrations and provides users access to the files that they need. Viruses and other malware also impact the ability of users to access resources.\nThe Official CompTIA Server+ Study Guide (Exam SK0-005) page 224."
    ],
    "url": "https://www.examtopics.com/discussions/comptia/view/87276-exam-sk0-005-topic-1-question-140-discussion/"
  },
  {
    "exam_code": "sk0-005",
    "topic": 1,
    "question_num": 141,
    "question": "Users are experiencing issues when trying to access resources on multiple servers. The servers are virtual and run on an ESX server. A systems administrator is investigating but is unable to connect to any of the virtual servers. When the administrator connects to the host, a purple screen with white letters appears. Which of the following troubleshooting steps should the administrator perform FIRST?",
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "Check the power supplies.",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "Review the log files.",
        "is_correct": true
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "Reinstall the ESX server.",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "Reseat the processors.",
        "is_correct": false
      }
    ],
    "suggested_answers": [
      "B"
    ],
    "answers": [
      "B. Review the log files.\n\nThe purple screen with white letters is commonly known as the \"purple screen of death\" (PSOD), which is an error screen that appears when there is a fatal error on an ESX/ESXi host. The first step in troubleshooting this issue should be to review the log files, which can provide additional information about the error that occurred.\n\nChecking the power supplies or reseating the processors would be unlikely to resolve this type of issue, as it is typically a software or configuration issue rather than a hardware issue. Reinstalling the ESX server would be a drastic step that should only be taken if all other troubleshooting steps have failed.",
      "Memory Failures\nMemory sticks are particularly susceptible to power fluctuations and static electricity. Failed or failing RAM causes OS instability. With Windows Server, this usually causes a Blue Screen to occur. This Windows crash screen provides some clues as to the problem on screen or via a memory dump file that can help identify problems leading up to the crash. If the server successfully restarts, check the log files for more information. On VMware ESXi systems, memory errors may trigger a Purple crash screen. On Linux servers, a kernel panic error may indicate problems with RAM.\n\nThe Official CompTIA Server+ Study Guide (Exam SK0-005) page 102.",
      "A purple screen of death (PSOD) is a diagnostic screen with white type on a purple background that's displayed when the VMkernel of a VMware ESXi host experiences a critical error, becomes inoperative and terminates any virtual machines (VMs) that are running.\n\nThe other answers could be the issue but are a bit drastic and definitely not something you do FIRST.\n\nhttps://www.techtarget.com/searchvmware/definition/Purple-Screen-of-Death-PSOD"
    ],
    "url": "https://www.examtopics.com/discussions/comptia/view/82639-exam-sk0-005-topic-1-question-141-discussion/"
  },
  {
    "exam_code": "sk0-005",
    "topic": 1,
    "question_num": 142,
    "question": "Joe, a technician, wants to configure a server's networking information so he will no longer need to maintain a list of names and IP addresses in a file on the server. Which of the following will work BEST to accomplish this task?",
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "DHCP",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "Hosts file",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "DNS",
        "is_correct": true
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "VLAN",
        "is_correct": false
      }
    ],
    "suggested_answers": [
      "C"
    ],
    "answers": [
      "C. \"List of names and ip#s in file on the computer\" is a hosts file. The next step up is DNS. Using a DHCP doesn't do -anything- without having a DNS for it to talk to.",
      "C. DNS\n\nDomain Name System (DNS) is the best solution to configure a server's networking information so that a list of names and IP addresses will no longer need to be maintained in a file on the server. DNS is a distributed system that translates domain names to IP addresses and vice versa. By using DNS, servers can be identified by their domain names instead of their IP addresses, and the IP addresses can be managed by the DNS server.\n\nDHCP can assign IP addresses to servers dynamically, but it does not eliminate the need for maintaining a list of names and IP addresses. A hosts file can be used to map names to IP addresses, but it needs to be maintained manually on each server. VLAN is a technology used to separate traffic on a network, but it is not related to managing server names and IP addresses.",
      "so sad to see A is the answer ..\nC definitely is the Correct one",
      "DNS resolves \"names and IP address\" things",
      "So sad. If they had just left out the reference to names, the DHCP answer would have won. Once you map IP addresses to names in a table, it's all over. DNS wins.",
      "DNS seems correct. to replace the host zone file with a list of ip address and host name"
    ],
    "url": "https://www.examtopics.com/discussions/comptia/view/82208-exam-sk0-005-topic-1-question-142-discussion/"
  },
  {
    "exam_code": "sk0-005",
    "topic": 1,
    "question_num": 143,
    "question": "A server administrator added a new drive to a server. However, the drive is not showing up as available. Which of the following does the administrator need to do to make the drive available?",
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "Partition the drive.",
        "is_correct": true
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "Create a new disk quota.",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "Configure the drive as dynamic.",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "Set the compression.",
        "is_correct": false
      }
    ],
    "suggested_answers": [
      "A"
    ],
    "answers": [
      "The administrator needs to partition the drive to make it available. Partitioning is the process of dividing a single hard drive into multiple logical drives. Once the drive is partitioned, it will be available for use.",
      "A. Partition the drive.\n\nThe administrator needs to partition the drive to make it available for use. Partitioning involves dividing the disk into one or more sections that can be used for storing data. Once the partition is created, the administrator can format it with a file system and assign a drive letter or mount point to make it accessible.\n\nCreating a new disk quota, configuring the drive as dynamic, or setting the compression are not necessary steps to make the drive available. Disk quotas are used to limit the amount of disk space that can be used by a user or group. Dynamic disks are used for advanced disk configurations, such as creating spanned or striped volumes. Compression is a feature that can be used to reduce the size of files on a disk.",
      "Agreed.",
      "Disk Management displays whether a disk is online (available), or offline.\n\nIn Windows, by default, all newly-discovered disks are brought online with read and write access. In Windows Server, by default, newly-discovered disks are brought online with read and write access unless they are on a shared bus (such as SCSI, iSCSI, Serial Attached SCSI, or Fibre Channel). Disks on a shared bus are offline the first time they are detected.\n\nYou would first need to bring online (available) then choose the partition and during this choose simple or spanned... etc. BCD are just options that you can choose later.\n\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/storage/disk-management/manage-disks"
    ],
    "url": "https://www.examtopics.com/discussions/comptia/view/82649-exam-sk0-005-topic-1-question-143-discussion/"
  },
  {
    "exam_code": "sk0-005",
    "topic": 1,
    "question_num": 144,
    "question": "Which of the following are measures that should be taken when a data breach occurs? (Choose two.)",
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "Restore the data from backup.",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "Disclose the incident.",
        "is_correct": true
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "Disable unnecessary ports.",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "Run an antivirus scan.",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "E",
        "text": "Identify the exploited vulnerability.",
        "is_correct": true
      },
      {
        "letter": "F",
        "text": "Move the data to a different location.",
        "is_correct": false
      }
    ],
    "suggested_answers": [
      "B",
      "E"
    ],
    "answers": [
      "B. Disclose the incident.\nE. Identify the exploited vulnerability.",
      "C is wrong - vulnerability may come from a necessary port.",
      "B. Disclose the incident.\nE. Identify the exploited vulnerability.\n\nWhen a data breach occurs, two important measures that should be taken are to disclose the incident and identify the exploited vulnerability. Disclosing the incident is important for transparency and to allow affected individuals to take steps to protect themselves. Identifying the exploited vulnerability can help to prevent future breaches and strengthen security measures.\n\nRestoring the data from backup may be necessary if the data was lost or corrupted during the breach, but it is not a measure that should be taken in all cases. Disabling unnecessary ports, running an antivirus scan, and moving the data to a different location may be necessary steps to prevent further damage or to secure the environment, but they are not measures that should be taken immediately following a data breach.",
      "Answer should be B and E.\nRegarding CompTIA Server+ SK0-005 Objective. Objective 3.4 Explain data security risks and mitigation strategies.\nIt's obvious they mentioned \"identification\" and \"disclosure\".",
      "IDENTIFICATION\nFirst, the organization must know when it has been breached. It might surprise you to know that in many cases organizations don't even know that a breach has occurred for weeks or months! Identifying breaches involves deep inspection of log files by experienced technicians to identify that data loss has occurred.\n\nDISCLOSURE\nIn many highly regulated industries, organizations are required by regulation or law to notify any users whose data has been disclosed. For example, in the healthcare field, the HIPAA Breach Notification Rule, 45 CFR §§ 164.400-414, requires HIPAA-covered entities and their business associates to provide notification following a breach of unsecured protected health information (PHI). As another example, all 50 states, the District of Columbia, Guam, Puerto Rico, and the Virgin Islands have enacted legislation requiring private or governmental entities to notify individuals of security breaches of information involving personally identifiable information (PII).",
      "The Department of Justice takes a very dim view towards those who lose customers' personal information and then wait to tell the authorities. I don't care if this one gets marked wrong on the exam, if I ever find myself in the middle of such a situation, I'm going to sing like a bird.",
      "Restoring from backup is unnecessary here as we dont know what exactly the breach affected.\nDisclose incident is AFTER all other steps have been taken. The question states WHEN a data breach occurs.\nMove the data to another location ... same issue. What data. Breach scope needs to be identified.\nIdentify the exploited vulnerability -- definitely\n\nDisable ports and running antivirus scan between these two... antivirus should already have been running and this wasn't exactly a virus. So disable unnecessary ports is the only other option that makes any sense. Although this should have been done before this point. What should happen is that you remove the affected systems from the network but do NOT shut them off.\n\nhttps://www.ftc.gov/business-guidance/resources/data-breach-response-guide-business"
    ],
    "url": "https://www.examtopics.com/discussions/comptia/view/82650-exam-sk0-005-topic-1-question-144-discussion/"
  },
  {
    "exam_code": "sk0-005",
    "topic": 1,
    "question_num": 145,
    "question": "A technician is building a lab to learn storage redundancy techniques. Which of the following is the MOST cost-effective method the technician can deploy?",
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "FCoE",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "Hardware RAID",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "Software RAID",
        "is_correct": true
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "JBOD",
        "is_correct": false
      }
    ],
    "suggested_answers": [
      "C"
    ],
    "answers": [
      "JBOD doesn't provide much redundancy and protection.",
      "D. JBOD (Just a Bunch Of Disks)\n\nJBOD is usually the most cost-effective option because it doesn't involve any specialized hardware or RAID controllers. It simply aggregates individual hard drives without any redundancy or striping. While it lacks redundancy and fault tolerance features, it can still be a suitable option for learning storage concepts and experimenting with redundancy techniques at a lower cost compared to the other options listed.\n\nFCoE (Fibre Channel over Ethernet), Hardware RAID, and Software RAID all involve additional hardware or software components, which can increase the overall cost.",
      "incorrect, software RAID accomplishes this with only 1 physical disk needed.",
      "C. Software RAID\n\nSoftware RAID is the most cost-effective method of deploying storage redundancy techniques in a lab environment. Software RAID uses the server's CPU and memory to manage the disk redundancy, and can be implemented without additional hardware or specialized equipment. Most operating systems have built-in support for software RAID, making it easy to set up and configure.\n\nHardware RAID is a more expensive option, as it requires specialized RAID controllers and storage devices that support RAID. FCoE (Fibre Channel over Ethernet) is a high-performance network protocol used to connect storage devices to servers, but it requires specialized network hardware and is not a cost-effective option for a lab environment. JBOD (Just a Bunch Of Disks) is a storage configuration where disks are presented to the operating system as individual drives, and it does not provide any redundancy or fault tolerance.",
      "Software RAID advantages:\n- Less expensive\n- Fewer components to manage\nDisadvantages:\n- Less flexible\n- Consumes system and OS resources\n\nHardware RAID advantages:\n- Faster\n- More flexible\n- Does not consume server resources\nDisadvantages:\n- More expensive\n- More components to manage\n- More complex\n\nThe Official CompTIA Server+ Study Guide (Exam SK0-005) page 114.",
      "JBOD is no data redundancy, and regardless of the number of disks in the system, if the data is spanned across the disks, the loss of a single disk means the loss of all data.",
      "Software RAID would be the most cost effective as it will use existing HDs to create the environment. Not to mention we are looking to learn storage redundancy. JBOD is the exact opposite of this.\n\nhttps://www.techtarget.com/searchstorage/definition/software-RAID-software-redundant-array-of-independent-disk#",
      "C is correct",
      "I think the correct answer is C. JBODs don't have redundancy."
    ],
    "url": "https://www.examtopics.com/discussions/comptia/view/81108-exam-sk0-005-topic-1-question-145-discussion/"
  },
  {
    "exam_code": "sk0-005",
    "topic": 1,
    "question_num": 146,
    "question": "A Linux administrator created a script that will run at startup. After successfully writing the script, the administrator received the following output when trying to execute the script: hash: ./startUp.sh: Permission denied\nWhich of the following commands would BEST resolve the error message?",
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "chmod +w startUp.sh",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "chmod 444 startUp.sh",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "chmod +x startUp.sh",
        "is_correct": true
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "chmod 466 startUp.sh",
        "is_correct": false
      }
    ],
    "suggested_answers": [
      "C"
    ],
    "answers": [
      "The error message \"Permission denied\" indicates that the script lacks execute permission. In Linux, to execute a script, it must have the execute permission bit set.\n\nThe correct command to resolve this error would be:\nC. chmod +x startUp.sh\n\nThis command adds the execute permission (+x) to the script file \"startUp.sh\", allowing it to be executed.",
      "x = excute\nr = read\nw = write",
      "C. chmod +x startUp.sh\n\nThe error message \"hash: ./startUp.sh: Permission denied\" indicates that the script cannot be executed because it does not have the executable permission. To resolve the issue, the administrator should use the chmod command to add the executable permission to the script. The correct command is \"chmod +x startUp.sh\". This will add the executable permission for the owner of the file, allowing the script to be executed at startup.\n\nThe other options do not address the issue of the script not having the executable permission. chmod +w would add the write permission, but this is not necessary for a script to run. chmod 444 would add read-only permission to the owner, group, and others, but again, this is not necessary for a script to run. chmod 466 would add read and write permission to the owner and group, but this still would not allow the script to be executed."
    ],
    "url": "https://www.examtopics.com/discussions/comptia/view/105459-exam-sk0-005-topic-1-question-146-discussion/"
  },
  {
    "exam_code": "sk0-005",
    "topic": 1,
    "question_num": 147,
    "question": "Due to a recent application migration, a company's current storage solution does not meet the necessary requirements for hosting data without impacting performance when the data is accessed in real time by multiple users. Which of the following is the BEST solution for this issue?",
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "Install local external hard drives for affected users.",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "Add extra memory to the server where data is stored.",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "Compress the data to increase available space.",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "Deploy a new Fibre Channel SAN solution.",
        "is_correct": true
      }
    ],
    "suggested_answers": [
      "D"
    ],
    "answers": [
      "D. Deploy a new Fibre Channel SAN solution.\n\nThe BEST solution for hosting data without impacting performance when accessed in real time by multiple users would be to deploy a new storage solution that can handle the requirements. A Fibre Channel SAN solution is a high-speed storage network that provides dedicated connectivity between servers and storage devices. This type of solution is designed for high-performance applications and can provide the necessary performance for multiple users accessing data in real time.\n\nInstalling external hard drives for affected users may provide additional storage, but it would not necessarily improve performance, and it would not address the root cause of the issue. Adding extra memory to the server may improve performance, but it would not necessarily be enough to handle the requirements, and it would not address the issue of storage capacity. Compressing the data to increase available space would not necessarily improve performance, and it would add additional overhead for decompression when accessing the data.",
      "Both SAN and NAS systems are network-based storage solutions aimed at providing multiple users 24/7 access to data on-premises and remotely. Here are some differences between the two approaches.\n\nType of network: NAS is connected to devices using a LAN or Ethernet network, while a SAN runs on high-speed Fibre channel."
    ],
    "url": "https://www.examtopics.com/discussions/comptia/view/82653-exam-sk0-005-topic-1-question-147-discussion/"
  },
  {
    "exam_code": "sk0-005",
    "topic": 1,
    "question_num": 148,
    "question": "Which of the following, if properly configured, would prevent a user from installing an OS on a server? (Choose two.)",
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "Administrator password",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "Group Policy Object",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "Root password",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "SELinux",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "E",
        "text": "Bootloader password",
        "is_correct": true
      },
      {
        "letter": "F",
        "text": "BIOS/UEFI password",
        "is_correct": true
      }
    ],
    "suggested_answers": [
      "E",
      "F"
    ],
    "answers": [
      "E. Bootloader password\nF. BIOS/UEFI password\n\nIf properly configured, a bootloader password or BIOS/UEFI password would prevent a user from installing an OS on a server. These passwords restrict access to the boot process, preventing unauthorized changes to the system.\n\nAn administrator password or root password would not necessarily prevent a user from installing an OS, as these passwords are used to control access to the operating system once it has already been installed.\n\nA Group Policy Object is a configuration tool used in Windows environments to manage security settings and other system configurations for groups of users and computers, but it would not prevent a user from installing an OS.\n\nSELinux is a security feature in Linux that provides mandatory access control for processes and files, but it would not prevent a user from installing an OS.",
      "Requiring a boot password upon execution of the boot loader will prevent an unauthorized user from entering boot parameters or changing the boot partition. This prevents users from weakening security (e.g. turning off SELinux at boot time).\n\nWhat is a UEFI password? A UEFI, or BIOS, password is a password that must be entered when the machine is powered on or rebooted in order to continue. Without the password the machine cannot be booted at all — even from external media — and no configuration changes to the UEFI or BIOS settings can be made.\n\nhttps://www.tenable.com/audits/items/CIS_Red_Hat_EL7_STIG_v2.0.0_STIG.audit:1ee8630516da75be0fabf64c11ab70c6\n\nhttps://askleo.com/how_do_i_remove_a_bios_password/#"
    ],
    "url": "https://www.examtopics.com/discussions/comptia/view/82657-exam-sk0-005-topic-1-question-148-discussion/"
  },
  {
    "exam_code": "sk0-005",
    "topic": 1,
    "question_num": 149,
    "question": "A server administrator is completing an OS installation for a new server. The administrator patches the server with the latest vendor-suggested software, configures DHCP, and verifies all network cables are properly connected in the IDF, but there is no network connectivity. Which of the following is the MOST likely reason for the lack of connectivity?",
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "The VLAN is improperly configured.",
        "is_correct": true
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "The DNS configuration is invalid.",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "The OS version is not compatible with the network switch vendor.",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "The HIDS is preventing the connection.",
        "is_correct": false
      }
    ],
    "suggested_answers": [
      "A"
    ],
    "answers": [
      "Don't know how technican patched server without network connection 😒",
      "An intermediate distribution frame (IDF) is a free-standing or wall-mounted rack for managing and interconnecting a telecommunications cable between end-user devices and the main distribution frame (MDF).\n\nDNS has nothing to do with LAN network connectivity. OS version and Switch vendor is not a thing. And the HIDS is detection not prevention. The only choice left is VLAN.\n\nhttps://www.techtarget.com/whatis/definition/intermediate-distribution-frame-IDF#"
    ],
    "url": "https://www.examtopics.com/discussions/comptia/view/82662-exam-sk0-005-topic-1-question-149-discussion/"
  },
  {
    "exam_code": "sk0-005",
    "topic": 1,
    "question_num": 150,
    "question": "Which of the following technologies would allow an administrator to build a software RAID on a Windows server?",
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "Logical volume management",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "Dynamic disk",
        "is_correct": true
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "GPT",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "UEFI",
        "is_correct": false
      }
    ],
    "suggested_answers": [
      "B"
    ],
    "answers": [
      "The technology that would allow an administrator to build a software RAID on a Windows server is B. Dynamic disk.\n\nDynamic disks provide software-based disk management that includes support for creating and managing RAID volumes. Logical volume management (A) is a technology used in Linux operating systems for dynamic disk management. GPT (C) is a partitioning scheme used for modern UEFI-based systems, while UEFI (D) is a firmware interface that replaced BIOS in modern systems.",
      "Once the OS is deployed, additional partitions may be created. Windows Server uses two different methods of organizing storage: Basic Disks and Dynamic Disks. By default, Windows uses the Basic Disk configuration, even though Dynamic Disk configurations are more flexible.\n\nDynamic disk attributes:\n- Manages simple, spanned, striped (RAID 0), mirrored (RAID 1), and striped with parity (RAID 5) volumes.\n- Simple and spanned volumes may be expanded to include more storage capacity.\n- Mirrored and RAID 5 configurations can be repaired after a failure.\n\nThe Official CompTIA Server+ Study Guide (Exam SK0-005) page 135.",
      "I agree with option B",
      "I think the correct answer is B.\nLVM is a technology used in UNIX."
    ],
    "url": "https://www.examtopics.com/discussions/comptia/view/81290-exam-sk0-005-topic-1-question-150-discussion/"
  },
  {
    "exam_code": "sk0-005",
