  {
    "exam_code": "sk0-005",
    "topic": 1,
    "question_num": 168,
    "question": "Which of the following load-balancing methods would be BEST described as each connection selecting the next path?",
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "Least connection",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "Source hashed",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "Most recently used",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "Round robin",
        "is_correct": true
      }
    ],
    "suggested_answers": [
      "D"
    ],
    "answers": [
      "Which of the following load-balancing methods would be BEST described as each connection selecting the next path?\nA. Least connection\nB. Source hashed\nC. Most recently used\nD. Round robin",
      "Round robin - Assigns connections in order\nMost recently used (MRU) - Attempts to use existing machines for the workload but can spin up new VMs if needed\nWeighted scheduling - Used when the server's hardware capabilities vary, allocating more connections to the more powerful servers\n\nThe Official CompTIA Server+ Study Guide (Exam SK0-005) page 244."
    ],
    "url": "https://www.examtopics.com/discussions/comptia/view/87292-exam-sk0-005-topic-1-question-168-discussion/"
  },
  {
    "exam_code": "sk0-005",
    "topic": 1,
    "question_num": 169,
    "question": "Which of the following should be configured in pairs on a server to provide network redundancy?",
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "MRU",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "SCP",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "DLP",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "CPU",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "E",
        "text": "NIC",
        "is_correct": true
      }
    ],
    "suggested_answers": [
      "E"
    ],
    "answers": [
      "Agreed. The answer is E",
      "Definitely E",
      "The network component that should be configured in pairs on a server to provide network redundancy is the NIC (Network Interface Card).\n\nNIC redundancy can be achieved by installing two NICs on a server and configuring them in a team or bond. This allows for the NICs to work in tandem and provide redundancy in case one of the NICs fails or experiences issues. If one NIC fails, the other NIC can continue to handle the network traffic, which helps to ensure that the server remains connected to the network and available.\n\nWhile other components listed may have redundancy options or configurations, they do not provide network redundancy in pairs on a server. MRU (Maximum Receive Unit) is a networking term that refers to the largest size of a packet that can be received on a network interface. SCP (Secure Copy Protocol) is a protocol used for securely transferring files between systems. DLP (Data Loss Prevention) is a strategy or technology used to prevent the loss or theft of sensitive data. CPU (Central Processing Unit) is a hardware component that processes instructions in a computer.",
      "NIC Teaming.",
      "Answer is E. It is called NIC Teaming.",
      "The NIC should be installed in Pairs to Provide Network redundancy. The key word is Network redundancy."
    ],
    "url": "https://www.examtopics.com/discussions/comptia/view/94854-exam-sk0-005-topic-1-question-169-discussion/"
  },
  {
    "exam_code": "sk0-005",
    "topic": 1,
    "question_num": 170,
    "question": "Which of the following BEST describes a security control that requires validating the user’s physical characteristics?",
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "OTP",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "Biometrics",
        "is_correct": true
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "RFID",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "Security cameras",
        "is_correct": false
      }
    ],
    "suggested_answers": [
      "B"
    ],
    "answers": [],
    "url": "https://www.examtopics.com/discussions/comptia/view/350383-exam-sk0-005-topic-1-question-170-discussion/"
  },
  {
    "exam_code": "sk0-005",
    "topic": 1,
    "question_num": 171,
    "question": "An administrator has set up a GPO to disable USB drive access on a company’s servers. The GPO is set up as a computer configuration policy and does not use security groups filtering. The administrator is testing to ensure the policy is working correctly. A server in the accounting OU works as expected, but a server in the sales OU is still allowing access to USB drives. The administrator runs the gpupdate /force command and tries again; however, the server still allows access to the USB drive.\n\nWhich of the following is the MOST likely cause?",
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "The WMI filter is not configured properly.",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "Item-level targeting is not configured properly.",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "The scope is not configured properly.",
        "is_correct": true
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "Loopback processing is not configured properly.",
        "is_correct": false
      }
    ],
    "suggested_answers": [
      "C"
    ],
    "answers": [
      "The MOST likely cause of the issue is that C. The scope is not configured properly. The GPO may not be linked to the sales OU or there may be another GPO with higher precedence that is overriding the settings. The administrator should check the Group Policy Management console to ensure that the GPO is linked to the sales OU and that there are no conflicting GPOs with higher precedence.",
      "C. The scope is not configured properly. The scope of a GPO determines which objects (such as users or computers) it applies to. Since the GPO is working as expected in the accounting OU but not in the sales OU, and there's no mention of security groups filtering being used, it suggests that the GPO may not be linked to the sales OU or the link is not active. This makes the scope of the GPO the most likely cause, as it might not be properly configured to include the sales OU or there could be an issue with how the GPO is linked to that OU.",
      "Going with B as stated. A nice detailed article on this can be found here \"https://www.policypak.com/resources/pp-blog/gpo-item-level-targeting/\""
    ],
    "url": "https://www.examtopics.com/discussions/comptia/view/97342-exam-sk0-005-topic-1-question-171-discussion/"
  },
  {
    "exam_code": "sk0-005",
    "topic": 1,
    "question_num": 172,
    "question": "A technician recently applied a patch to a working server. After rebooting, the technician notices the server is unable to connect to a nearby database server. The technician validates a connection can be made to the database from another host.\n\nWhich of the following is the best NEXT step to restore connectivity?",
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "Enable HIDS.",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "Change the service account permissions.",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "Check the firewall rules.",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "Roll back the applied patch.",
        "is_correct": true
      }
    ],
    "suggested_answers": [
      "D"
    ],
    "answers": [
      "C. Check the firewall rules.\n\nYes, the server worked before the patch, but we have no indication what kind of patch it was. This is a troubleshooting question. So follow the process. You don't just jump straight to remove the patch without investigating possible causes.\nIt doesn't say \"change\" the firewall rules, it says \"Check\" the firewall rules. This is a low effort step that will yield information, even if nothing has changed. Assume host based firewall on the server could have been modified by the patch. What if the firewall was disabled, or enabled by the patch? Antivirus software updates and uninstalls have been known to disable network cards, and similar such anomalies.",
      "I am going with D as well",
      "Definitely D. Have to bring server back up before troubleshooting.",
      "Going with answer D",
      "I think the answer is D because Server had network connectivity before the patch."
    ],
    "url": "https://www.examtopics.com/discussions/comptia/view/94855-exam-sk0-005-topic-1-question-172-discussion/"
  },
  {
    "exam_code": "sk0-005",
    "topic": 1,
    "question_num": 173,
    "question": "Users report they are unable to access an application after a recent third-party patch update. The physical server that is hosting the application keeps crashing on reboot. Although the update was installed directly from the manufacturer’s support website as recommended, it has now been recalled and removed from the website, as the update unintentionally installed unauthorized software after a reboot.\n\nWhich of the following steps should the administrator perform to restore access to the application while minimizing downtime? (Choose two.)",
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "Uninstall recent updates.",
        "is_correct": true
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "Reimage the server with a different OS.",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "Run a port scan to verify open ports.",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "Enable a GPO to uninstall the update.",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "E",
        "text": "Scan and remove any malware.",
        "is_correct": true
      },
      {
        "letter": "F",
        "text": "Reformat the server and restore the image from the latest backup.",
        "is_correct": false
      }
    ],
    "suggested_answers": [
      "A",
      "E"
    ],
    "answers": [
      "To restore access to the application while minimizing downtime, the administrator should A. Uninstall recent updates and E. Scan and remove any malware. Uninstalling the recent update that caused the issue will likely resolve the problem with the server crashing on reboot. Scanning and removing any malware will ensure that the unauthorized software installed by the update is removed from the system.",
      "A. Uninstall recent updates.\nE. Scan and remove any malware.\n\nThe first step the administrator should take is to uninstall the recent update, as it is causing the server to crash and preventing access to the application. This will help to restore access to the application without any further downtime.\n\nIn addition, the administrator should also scan and remove any malware that may have been inadvertently installed as a result of the unauthorized software that was installed by the recent update. This will help to ensure that the server is secure and prevent any future issues.\n\nOptions B, C, D, and F are not necessary or recommended for this situation. Reimaging the server with a different OS or reformatting the server and restoring from backup would result in significant downtime and may not address the root cause of the issue. Running a port scan and enabling a GPO to uninstall the update are not necessary for resolving this issue."
    ],
    "url": "https://www.examtopics.com/discussions/comptia/view/105963-exam-sk0-005-topic-1-question-173-discussion/"
  },
  {
    "exam_code": "sk0-005",
    "topic": 1,
    "question_num": 174,
    "question": "An administrator is only able to log on to a server with a local account. The server has been successfully joined to the domain and can ping other servers by IP address.\n\nWhich of the following locally defined settings is MOST likely misconfigured?",
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "DHCP",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "WINS",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "DNS",
        "is_correct": true
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "TCP",
        "is_correct": false
      }
    ],
    "suggested_answers": [
      "C"
    ],
    "answers": [
      "C. DNS (Domain Name System)\nDNS translates domain names to IP addresses. For a server to authenticate using domain credentials, it must be able to resolve the domain controller's name to an IP address. If DNS settings are incorrect and the server cannot resolve the domain controller's name, it would not be able to authenticate domain accounts, even though it could still ping other servers by their IP addresses. This makes DNS the most likely candidate for the misconfiguration causing the issue.",
      "C. DNS\n\nSince the administrator is only able to log on to the server with a local account, it is likely that the server is having difficulty resolving domain names to IP addresses. This suggests that there may be a misconfiguration in the Domain Name System (DNS) settings on the server.\n\nDNS is responsible for translating domain names into IP addresses, which allows computers to locate resources on the network. If the DNS settings on the server are incorrect, the server may not be able to locate domain resources, including domain controllers, which would prevent the administrator from logging on with a domain account.\n\nDHCP (A) is responsible for assigning IP addresses to devices on the network, and WINS (B) is an older technology used to resolve NetBIOS names to IP addresses. Neither of these services are directly related to logging on to a server with a domain account. TCP (D) is a protocol used to establish connections between devices on a network, and is not likely to be the source of the issue in this scenario.",
      "should D, I think.\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/troubleshoot/windows-server/identity/cant-sign-the-domain"
    ],
    "url": "https://www.examtopics.com/discussions/comptia/view/105399-exam-sk0-005-topic-1-question-174-discussion/"
  },
  {
    "exam_code": "sk0-005",
    "topic": 1,
    "question_num": 175,
    "question": "A server technician downloaded new firmware from the manufacturer’s website. The technician then attempted to install the firmware on the server, but the installation failed, stating the file is potentially corrupt.\n\nWhich of the following should the technician have checked prior to installing the firmware?",
