  {
    "exam_code": "sk0-005",
    "topic": 1,
    "question_num": 177,
    "question": "Which of the following BEST describes overprovisioning in a virtual server environment?",
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "Committing more virtual resources to virtual machines than there are physical resources present",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "Installing more physical hardware than is necessary to run the virtual environment to allow for future expansion",
        "is_correct": true
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "Allowing a virtual machine to utilize more resources than are allocated to it based on the server load",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "Ensuring there are enough physical resources to sustain the complete virtual environment in the event of a host failure",
        "is_correct": false
      }
    ],
    "suggested_answers": [
      "B"
    ],
    "answers": [
      "The correct answer is:\n\n✅ A. Committing more virtual resources to virtual machines than there are physical resources present\n\nExplanation:\nOverprovisioning refers to the practice of allocating more virtual resources (such as CPU, RAM, or storage) to virtual machines (VMs) than the physical host actually has available. In a virtualized environment, hypervisors can sometimes allow VMs to be allocated more resources than are physically available, with the assumption that not all VMs will require their full allocation at the same time. However, if too many VMs demand their full resources simultaneously, it can lead to performance issues or even failures.\n\nThis practice can work if the overall load is well managed, but it can be risky if resource contention becomes an issue.",
      "It's (B). Per the CompTIA Server+ Student Guide:\n\nOverprovision\nSystems administrators must ensure that the host server has enough physical hardware resources to meet the needs of the VMs. Therefore, the server is equipped with more hardware resources than the anticipated use requires.",
      "The BEST description of overprovisioning in a virtual server environment is:\nA. Committing more virtual resources to virtual machines than there are physical resources present\nThis accurately describes the concept of overprovisioning in virtualization. Overprovisioning refers to allocating more resources to virtual machines collectively than the physical host actually has available",
      "I chose A, because B won't lead to an actual issue with the virtual servers.\nUsing a hard drive as an analogy, if I have 1 TB of data to store and a 2TB drive, I will be fine. If I have 1 TB of data to store, but only a 500 GB drive, I will be over capacity.\nOver provisioning then in this case, means you have more virtual servers than the physical hardware can support. Or to go back to our analogy, you only have 500GB of capacity, but you are trying to store 1TB of data.\n\nThat said, the question is poorly worded.",
      "poorly worded. if you're overprovisioning the VMs it is A, if you're overprovisioning the host it is B",
      "They want A for the answer btw.",
      "To provide more than is necessary.",
      "overprovisioning = To provide more than is necessary.",
      "Correct Answer: B\nSystems administrators must ensure that the host server has enough physical\nhardware resources to meet the needs of the VMs. Therefore, the server is\nequipped with more hardware resources than the anticipated use requires.",
      "Which of the following BEST describes overprovisioning in a virtual server environment?\n\nA. Committing more virtual resources to virtual machines than there are physical resources present\nB. Installing more physical hardware than is necessary to run the virtual environment to allow for future expansion\nC. Allowing a virtual machine to utilize more resources than are allocated to it based on the server load\nD. Ensuring there are enough physical resources to sustain the complete virtual environment in the event of a host failure"
    ],
    "url": "https://www.examtopics.com/discussions/comptia/view/106495-exam-sk0-005-topic-1-question-177-discussion/"
  },
  {
    "exam_code": "sk0-005",
    "topic": 1,
    "question_num": 178,
    "question": "An administrator is alerted to a hardware failure in a mission-critical server. The alert states that two drives have failed. The administrator notes the drives are in different RAID 1 arrays, and both are hot-swappable.\n\nWhich of the following steps will be the MOST efficient?",
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "Replace one drive, wait for a rebuild, and replace the next drive.",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "Shut down the server and replace the drives.",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "Replace both failed drives at the same time.",
        "is_correct": true
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "Replace all the drives in both degraded arrays.",
        "is_correct": false
      }
    ],
    "suggested_answers": [
      "C"
    ],
    "answers": [
      "why wait",
      "don't cry when the controller fails or one/both arrays fail to rebuild. Real world answer is A.",
      "The most efficient step would be to replace both failed drives at the same time. This will allow for the quickest recovery time since both drives have failed and need to be replaced to restore data redundancy.\n\nReplacing one drive at a time, waiting for a rebuild, and then replacing the next drive will increase the risk of data loss if another drive fails during the rebuild process. Shutting down the server to replace the drives will result in extended downtime. Replacing all the drives in both degraded arrays is not necessary, as only the two failed drives need to be replaced.",
      "Different Raid Arrays"
    ],
    "url": "https://www.examtopics.com/discussions/comptia/view/105989-exam-sk0-005-topic-1-question-178-discussion/"
  },
  {
    "exam_code": "sk0-005",
    "topic": 1,
    "question_num": 179,
    "question": "A systems administrator is trying to determine why users in the human resources department cannot access an application server. The administrator reviews the application logs but does not see any attempts by the users to access the application.\n\nWhich of the following is preventing the users from accessing the application server?",
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "NAT",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "ICMP",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "VLAN",
        "is_correct": true
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "NIDS",
        "is_correct": false
      }
    ],
    "suggested_answers": [
      "C"
    ],
    "answers": [
      "C for me",
      "If the users in the human resources department cannot access an application server and there are no logs indicating an attempt to access the application, it suggests there might be network segmentation or isolation in place. The most common method to isolate different departments or sections of a network is using VLANs (Virtual Local Area Networks).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is:\n\nC. VLAN",
      "If the application logs do not show any attempts by users to access the application server, it is likely that some network-related issue is preventing users from accessing the server.\n\nOut of the options given, the most likely culprit is VLAN (Virtual Local Area Network).",
      "Can't be ICMP",
      "B. ICMP\n\nThe fact that the administrator is not seeing any attempts by the users to access the application in the application logs suggests that the issue may be related to network connectivity. ICMP (Internet Control Message Protocol) is a network protocol used to test for connectivity between two devices on a network. If ICMP traffic is being blocked by a firewall or other network device, it could prevent the human resources department users from accessing the application server.\n\nNAT (Network Address Translation) (A) is a technique used to remap IP addresses in a network packet, and would not be directly related to the issue of users not being able to access the application server. VLAN (Virtual Local Area Network) (C) is a logical group of network devices that share a common broadcast domain, and would also not be directly related to the issue. NIDS (Network Intrusion Detection System) (D) is a security technology used to detect network attacks and would not be directly related to the issue of users not being able to access the application server.",
      "they aren't trying to ping it... only a VLAN would explain why their connection attempts aren't logged."
    ],
    "url": "https://www.examtopics.com/discussions/comptia/view/105964-exam-sk0-005-topic-1-question-179-discussion/"
  },
  {
    "exam_code": "sk0-005",
    "topic": 1,
    "question_num": 180,
    "question": "An administrator discovers a Bash script file has the following permissions set in octal notation:\n\n777\n\nWhich of the following is the MOST appropriate command to ensure only the root user can modify and execute the script?",
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "chmod go-rwx",
        "is_correct": true
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "chmod u=rwx",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "chmod u+wx",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "chmod g-rwx",
        "is_correct": false
      }
    ],
    "suggested_answers": [
      "A"
    ],
    "answers": [
      "(A) chmod go-rwx\n\nRemoving the rwx permissions for groups and owners (go) would make it so ONLY the root user can modify and execute the script.\n*running \"chmod u=rwx\" would change nothing and script's permissions would STILL be 777.",
      "Ignore last\n\n777\n\nWhich of the following is the MOST appropriate command to ensure only the root user can modify and execute the script?\n\nA. chmod go-rwx\nB. chmod u=rwx\nC. chmod u+wx\nD. chmod g-rwx",
      "chmod go-rwx: This command would remove read, write, and execute permissions for group and others but would leave the owner's permissions unchanged. If the owner is not root, this does not achieve the desired outcome.",
      "B. chmod u=rwx\nThis command sets the permissions for the user (owner) to read, write, and execute (rwx) while removing all permissions for the group and others. After executing this command, the permissions would be changed from 777 to 700, meaning only the root user can access and modify the script.",
      "A. chmod go-rwx\n\n\"chmod\" is the command used to change the file's mode bits.\n\"go-rwx\" specifies that both group (g) and others (o) should have read (r), write (w), and execute (x) permissions removed (-).\n\nThis command removes the read, write, and execute permissions from both the group and others, leaving only the user (assuming the root is the owner) with the ability to read, write, and execute the script. This makes the script's permissions 700 in octal notation, ensuring that only the root user (assuming root owns the file) can modify and execute the script.",
      "But we don't have the g=,o= in the available options. Using just \"u=rwx\" is not going to remove the permissions from group and the others therefore we should remove it with the A: chmod go-rwx",
      "Agree with Mareo B",
      "The octal notation \"777\" represents full permissions (read, write, and execute) for the owner, group, and others. To ensure only the root user can modify and execute the script, we need to remove write and execute permissions for group and others while keeping the same permissions for the owner. Therefore, the most appropriate command would be:\n\nB. chmod u=rwx,g=,o= script_file\n\nThis command sets the owner's permissions to read, write, and execute (u=rwx) while removing all permissions for the group (g=) and others (o=), effectively restricting modification and execution to the root user only.",
      "Nope, it's A which removes read write and execute from group and others."
    ],
    "url": "https://www.examtopics.com/discussions/comptia/view/105922-exam-sk0-005-topic-1-question-180-discussion/"
  },
  {
    "exam_code": "sk0-005",
    "topic": 1,
    "question_num": 181,
    "question": "A security technician generated a public/private key pair on a server. The technician needs to copy the key pair to another server on a different subnet.\n\nWhich of the following is the MOST secure method to copy the keys?",
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "HTTP",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "FTP",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "SCP",
        "is_correct": true
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "USB",
        "is_correct": false
      }
    ],
    "suggested_answers": [
      "C"
    ],
    "answers": [
      "Secure Copy Protocol (commonly used in Linux/Networking)",
      "C: SCP (Secure Copy Protocol).\n\nSCP (Secure Copy Protocol) is a secure method for transferring files between hosts on a network. It uses SSH (Secure Shell) for data transfer and provides encryption and authentication. This ensures that the keys are transferred securely without being intercepted or compromised.",
      "C. SCP. SCP uses SSH, HTTP and FTP are unencrypted."
    ],
    "url": "https://www.examtopics.com/discussions/comptia/view/127691-exam-sk0-005-topic-1-question-181-discussion/"
  },
  {
    "exam_code": "sk0-005",
    "topic": 1,
    "question_num": 182,
    "question": "A server administrator purchased a single license key to use for all the new servers that will be imaged this year. Which of the following MOST likely refers to the licensing type that will be used?",
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "Per socket",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "Open-source",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "Per concurrent user",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "Volume",
        "is_correct": true
      }
    ],
    "suggested_answers": [
      "D"
    ],
    "answers": [
      "Volume licensing is a method whereby a company or organization purchases software licenses for multiple computers or servers in bulk. Volume purchases are usually less expensive than individual purchases.\n\nThe problem statement states that the server administrator purchased “a single license key to use for all the new servers that will be imaged this year.\" This is a typical volume license use case."
    ],
    "url": "https://www.examtopics.com/discussions/comptia/view/147480-exam-sk0-005-topic-1-question-182-discussion/"
  },
  {
    "exam_code": "sk0-005",
    "topic": 1,
    "question_num": 183,
    "question": "A technician is attempting to log in to a Linux server as root but cannot remember the administrator password. Which of the following is the LEAST destructive method of resetting the administrator password?",
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "Boot using a Linux live CD and mount the hard disk to /mnt. Change to the /mnt/etc directory. Edit the passwd file found in that directory.",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "Reinstall the OS in overlay mode. Reset the root password from the install GUI screen.",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "Adjust the GRUB boot parameters to boot into single-user mode. Run passwd from the command prompt.",
        "is_correct": true
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "Boot using a Linux live CD and mount the hard disk to /mnt. SCP the /etc directory from a known accessible server to /mnt/etc.",
        "is_correct": false
      }
    ],
    "suggested_answers": [
      "C"
    ],
    "answers": [
      "A. While editing the passwd file could reset the root password, it's prone to error and could potentially corrupt the system if not done correctly.\n\nB. Reinstalling the OS in overlay mode is unnecessary and involves reinstalling the entire operating system, which is very destructive and time-consuming for just resetting a password.\n\n***C. Booting into single-user mode via GRUB and using the passwd command to reset the password is a standard method for resetting passwords on Linux systems.\n\nD. Using SCP to copy the /etc directory from a known accessible server to the mounted hard disk could overwrite crucial system files and potentially cause system instability or corruption.",
      "Among the options provided, the least destructive method to reset the administrator password without losing data or altering major system configurations is:\n\nC. Adjust the GRUB boot parameters to boot into single-user mode. Run passwd from the command prompt.\n\nSingle-user mode allows you to log in to the system as the root user without requiring a password. Once in single-user mode, the passwd command can be used to change the root password.",
      "The least destructive method of resetting the administrator password would be option C, adjusting the GRUB boot parameters to boot into single-user mode and running passwd from the command prompt. This method allows the technician to reset the root password without making any changes to the system's configuration files or reinstalling the operating system.\n\nOption A involves editing the system's passwd file, which could potentially cause issues with user accounts and permissions.\n\nOption B involves reinstalling the OS, which is a very destructive method and would result in data loss and a complete reset of the system.\n\nOption D involves copying system configuration files from another server, which could potentially introduce new problems and is not a recommended method for resetting the root password."
    ],
    "url": "https://www.examtopics.com/discussions/comptia/view/106504-exam-sk0-005-topic-1-question-183-discussion/"
  },
  {
    "exam_code": "sk0-005",
    "topic": 1,
    "question_num": 184,
    "question": "A technician noted the RAID hard drives were functional while troubleshooting a motherboard failure. The technician installed a spare motherboard with similar specifications and used the original components.\n\nWhich of the following should the technician do to restore operations with minimal downtime?",
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "Reinstall the OS and programs.",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "Configure old drives to RAID.",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "Reconfigure the RAID.",
        "is_correct": true
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "Install from backup.",
        "is_correct": false
      }
    ],
    "suggested_answers": [
      "C"
    ],
    "answers": [
      "C. Reconfigure the RAID",
      "C. Reconfigure the RAID.\n\nWhen a motherboard is replaced, especially if it's similar in specifications, the RAID configuration may be affected. RAID (Redundant Array of Independent Disks) configurations are often tied to the specific hardware on which they were configured. Reconfiguring the RAID ensures that the array is recognized and functions correctly with the new motherboard, minimizing downtime and allowing the system to operate with the original set of components."
    ],
    "url": "https://www.examtopics.com/discussions/comptia/view/127694-exam-sk0-005-topic-1-question-184-discussion/"
  },
  {
    "exam_code": "sk0-005",
    "topic": 1,
    "question_num": 185,
    "question": "A server administrator is trying to determine the cause of a slowdown on a database server. Upon investigation, the administrator determines the issue is in the storage subsystem.\n\nWhich of the following will MOST likely resolve this issue?",
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "Increasing IOPS by implementing flash storage",
        "is_correct": true
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "Implementing deduplication on the storage",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "Extending capacity by installing a 4TB SATA disk",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "Reformatting the disk as FAT32",
        "is_correct": false
      }
    ],
    "suggested_answers": [
      "A"
    ],
    "answers": [
      "A. Increasing IOPS by implementing flash storage.",
      "The most likely option to resolve a slowdown in the storage subsystem, especially for a database server, would be:\n\nA. Increasing IOPS (Input/Output Operations Per Second) by implementing flash storage.\n\nFlash storage (like SSDs) significantly improves IOPS compared to traditional spinning disks, which can greatly benefit the performance of database operations."
    ],
    "url": "https://www.examtopics.com/discussions/comptia/view/123801-exam-sk0-005-topic-1-question-185-discussion/"
  },
  {
    "exam_code": "sk0-005",
    "topic": 1,
    "question_num": 186,
    "question": "A server administrator made a change in a server’s BIOS in an attempt to fix an issue with the OS not turning on. However, the change did not successfully correct the issue.\n\nWhich of the following should the server administrator do NEXT?",
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "Reinstall the server OS in repair mode while maintaining the data.",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "Flash the BIOS with the most recent version.",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "Reverse the latest change made to the server and reboot.",
        "is_correct": true
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "Restart the server into safe mode and roll back changes.",
        "is_correct": false
      }
    ],
    "suggested_answers": [
      "C"
    ],
    "answers": [
      "A server administrator made a change in a server’s BIOS in an attempt to fix an issue with the OS not turning on. However, the change did not successfully correct the issue.\n\nWhich of the following should the server administrator do NEXT?\n\nA. Reinstall the server OS in repair mode while maintaining the data.\nB. Flash the BIOS with the most recent version.\nC. Reverse the latest change made to the server and reboot.\nD. Restart the server into safe mode and roll back changes."
    ],
    "url": "https://www.examtopics.com/discussions/comptia/view/106508-exam-sk0-005-topic-1-question-186-discussion/"
  },
  {
    "exam_code": "sk0-005",
    "topic": 1,
    "question_num": 187,
    "question": "Which of the following BEST describes a guarantee of the amount of time it will take to restore a downed service?",
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "RTO",
        "is_correct": true
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "SLA",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "MTBF",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "MTTR",
        "is_correct": false
      }
    ],
    "suggested_answers": [
      "A"
    ],
    "answers": [
      "The guarantee of the amount of time it will take to restore a downed service is typically referred to as the \"RTO\" or \"Recovery Time Objective.\" RTO is the amount of time within which a system, application, or service must be restored after a disruption in order to avoid unacceptable consequences associated with a break in business continuity.\n\nOption A, RTO, is the correct answer.\n\nOption B, SLA (Service Level Agreement), is an agreement between a service provider and its customers that defines the level of service, availability, and other aspects of the service that the provider agrees to deliver.\n\nOption C, MTBF (Mean Time Between Failures), is the average time that elapses between failures of a system, component, or product.\n\nOption D, MTTR (Mean Time To Repair), is the average time it takes to repair a system, component, or product after a failure has occurred.",
      "Going with (B), a Service Level Agreement (SLA) is a set definitive contract of expectations between provider and customer that \"guarantees\" recovery times in the event of a disaster.\n* An RTO is more of an internal target for the service provider.",
      "D. MTTR (Mean Time To Restore)\n\nA. RTO (Recovery Time Objective) refers to the targeted duration within which a service or system must be restored after a disaster or failure, but it doesn't specifically quantify the actual time it takes to restore the service.\nB. SLA (Service Level Agreement) is a contract between a service provider and a customer, specifying the level of service to be provided, but it may include various metrics beyond just restoration time.\nC. MTBF (Mean Time Between Failures) represents the average time elapsed between failures of a system or component, which is different from the time taken to restore the service after a failure.\n***D. MTTR represents the average time it takes to repair a failed system or component and restore it to normal operation. It's an important metric in understanding the reliability and maintainability of a system.",
      "MTTR is Mean Time To Repair, so you are wrong. It's A."
    ],
    "url": "https://www.examtopics.com/discussions/comptia/view/105966-exam-sk0-005-topic-1-question-187-discussion/"
  },
  {
    "exam_code": "sk0-005",
    "topic": 1,
    "question_num": 188,
    "question": "An administrator is investigating several unexpected documents and video files that recently appeared in a network share. The administrator checks the properties of the files and sees the author’s name on the documents is not a company employee. The administrator questions the other users, but no one knows anything about the files. The administrator then checks the log files and discovers the FTP protocol was used to copy the files to the server.\n\nWhich of the following needs to be done to prevent this from happening again?",
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "Implement data loss prevention.",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "Configure intrusion detection.",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "Turn on User Account Control.",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "Disable anonymous access.",
        "is_correct": true
      }
    ],
    "suggested_answers": [
      "D"
    ],
    "answers": [
      "D. Disable anonymous access.\n\nThe fact that the FTP protocol was used to copy the files to the server suggests that the network share has anonymous access enabled. Disabling anonymous access would require users to authenticate before accessing the share, which would prevent unauthorized access and file transfers from external sources.\n\nData loss prevention (A) and intrusion detection (B) are both important security measures, but they may not necessarily prevent unauthorized access to a network share. User Account Control (C) is a feature in Windows that helps prevent unauthorized changes to a computer, but it is not directly related to preventing unauthorized access to a network share. Therefore, the best option in this scenario would be to disable anonymous access."
    ],
    "url": "https://www.examtopics.com/discussions/comptia/view/106509-exam-sk0-005-topic-1-question-188-discussion/"
  },
  {
    "exam_code": "sk0-005",
    "topic": 1,
    "question_num": 189,
    "question": "A technician is monitoring a server and notices there is only one NIC plugged in, but the server has two. The NIC is oversaturated, and the technician would like to increase the available bandwidth.\n\nWhich of the following solutions would be the BEST option to increase the speed of this NIC?",
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "Link aggregation",
        "is_correct": true
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "Heartbeat",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "Most recently used",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "Active-active",
        "is_correct": false
      }
    ],
    "suggested_answers": [
      "A"
    ],
    "answers": [
      "Link aggregation, also known as port trunking or channel bonding, is a networking technique that combines multiple physical Ethernet links into a single logical link. This process increases bandwidth, improves fault tolerance, and provides redundancy in case one link fails. It's commonly used in scenarios like data centers or between switches and servers where high availability and performance are critical."
    ],
    "url": "https://www.examtopics.com/discussions/comptia/view/303113-exam-sk0-005-topic-1-question-189-discussion/"
  },
  {
    "exam_code": "sk0-005",
    "topic": 1,
    "question_num": 190,
    "question": "An administrator is troubleshooting performance issues on a server that was recently upgraded. The administrator met with users/stakeholders and documented recent changes in an effort to determine whether the server is better or worse since the changes.\n\nWhich of the following would BEST help answer the server performance question?",
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "Server performance thresholds",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "A server baseline",
        "is_correct": true
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "A hardware compatibility list",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "An application service-level agreement",
        "is_correct": false
      }
    ],
    "suggested_answers": [
      "B"
    ],
    "answers": [
      "A baseline is the ideal or standard configuration for that node. It is the configuration against which you want to judge that node going forward."
    ],
    "url": "https://www.examtopics.com/discussions/comptia/view/303114-exam-sk0-005-topic-1-question-190-discussion/"
