  {
    "exam_code": "sk0-005",
    "topic": 1,
    "question_num": 265,
    "question": "Joe, a user in the IT department, cannot save changes to a sensitive file on a Linux server. An is -la shows the following listing:\n-rw-r--r 1 Ann IT 6780 12 June 2019 filename\n\nWhich of the following commands would BEST enable the server technician to allow Joe to have access without granting excessive access to others?",
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "chmod 777 filename",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "chown Joe filename",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "chmod g+w filename",
        "is_correct": true
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "chgrp IT filename",
        "is_correct": false
      }
    ],
    "suggested_answers": [
      "C"
    ],
    "answers": [
      "Option A (chmod 777 filename): This command would grant read, write, and execute permissions to everyone (owner, group, and others) on the file.\nThis is not the way.\n\nOption B (chown Joe filename): This command changes the owner of the file to Joe.\nThis is not the way.\n\nOption C (chmod g+w filename): This command adds write permission for the group associated with the file. Since Joe belongs to the \"IT\" group (as indicated in the file listing), this command would allow him to write to the file without granting excessive access to others. This is the correct approach because it specifically targets the group, providing the necessary access without compromising security for other users. This is the way.\n\nOption D (chgrp IT filename): This command changes the group ownership of the file to \"IT.\" This is not the way.",
      "What surf said, running D would be redundant and change nothing. C is correct."
    ],
    "url": "https://www.examtopics.com/discussions/comptia/view/135836-exam-sk0-005-topic-1-question-265-discussion/"
  },
  {
    "exam_code": "sk0-005",
    "topic": 1,
    "question_num": 266,
    "question": "Which of the following is used for fail over, providing access to all the services currently in use by an organization without having to physically move any servers or employees?",
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "The cloud",
        "is_correct": true
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "A cold site",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "A warm site",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "An emergency operations center",
        "is_correct": false
      }
    ],
    "suggested_answers": [
      "A"
    ],
    "answers": [
      "The cloud is used for failover to ensure continuous access to services without physically moving servers or employees. Cloud services, provided by platforms like AWS, Azure, or Google Cloud, have distributed data centers and can automatically redirect traffic in case of server or data center issues. This contrasts with options like cold and warm sites, which require manual intervention and may involve moving servers, and an emergency operations center, which is not designed for failover but for managing emergency response activities."
    ],
    "url": "https://www.examtopics.com/discussions/comptia/view/128495-exam-sk0-005-topic-1-question-266-discussion/"
  },
  {
    "exam_code": "sk0-005",
    "topic": 1,
    "question_num": 267,
    "question": "Which of the following BEST measures how much downtime an organization can tolerate during an unplanned outage?",
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "SLA",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "BIA",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "RTO",
        "is_correct": true
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "MTTR",
        "is_correct": false
      }
    ],
    "suggested_answers": [
      "C"
    ],
    "answers": [
      "C. RTO (Recovery Time Objective)\n\nRTO (Recovery Time Objective) measures the maximum tolerable duration of time that a business process can be unavailable or disrupted after a failure or disaster occurs. It represents the time within which systems and services must be recovered after an outage to avoid significant consequences."
    ],
    "url": "https://www.examtopics.com/discussions/comptia/view/135837-exam-sk0-005-topic-1-question-267-discussion/"
  },
  {
    "exam_code": "sk0-005",
    "topic": 1,
    "question_num": 268,
    "question": "The management team at a healthcare organization is concerned about being able to access the daily vital records if there is an IT disaster that causes both servers and the network to be offline. Which of the following backup types can the organization use to mitigate this risk?",
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "Tape",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "Cloud",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "Disk",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "Print",
        "is_correct": true
      }
    ],
    "suggested_answers": [
      "D"
    ],
    "answers": [
      "D. Print\n\nNo servers and no network means we failover to paper records and the whiteboard.",
      "If servers and network are down disk or print may be available. I think many health care providers have paper as a backup so Print seems most likely to me",
      "The answer is D. If there is an IT disaster that causes both servers to go down. Print documents will be the only thing avaiable due to the fact that all other options need a computer",
      "Tricky question. If servers and network is down, how do we access \"VITAL\" information? Only way is print.\n\nI have worked in organizations that print out critical and vital information and store them in safe's just in case the network and servers go down.",
      "The answer is B. Cloud.\nThis is a type of backup that can mitigate the risk of being unable to access the daily vital records if there is an IT disaster that causes both servers and the network to be offline. A cloud backup means that the data is copied and stored on a remote cloud server that can be accessed over the internet. A cloud backup offers the benefits of off-site data protection, scalability, reliability, and cost-effectiveness, but may have less control, security, and performance than other backup types.",
      "After a second look, the cloud can't be accessed in case the network goes down.\nBut Disks, Tapes, and Print files won't be accessible in case of a disaster either.\nSuch a stupid question!",
      "Exactly, I also vote for B as it would be the easiest option in case of such disaster. Someone would create a data hotspot and access the records that way. I dont see how are you going to read the data from tapes if your infra is down",
      "\"IT disaster\" so print would be available."
    ],
    "url": "https://www.examtopics.com/discussions/comptia/view/112954-exam-sk0-005-topic-1-question-268-discussion/"
  },
  {
    "exam_code": "sk0-005",
    "topic": 1,
    "question_num": 269,
    "question": "A server administrator notices the /var/log/audit/audit.log file on a Linux server is rotating too frequently. The administrator would like to decrease the number of times the log rotates without losing any of the information in the logs. Which of the following should the administrator configure?",
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "Increase the audit.log file size in the appropriate configuration file.",
        "is_correct": true
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "Decrease the duration of the log rotate cycle for the audit.log file.",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "Remove the log rotate directive from the audit.log file configuration.",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "Move the audit.log files to a remote syslog server.",
        "is_correct": false
      }
    ],
    "suggested_answers": [
      "A"
    ],
    "answers": [
      "B is the exact OPPOSITE of what is being requested. By decreasing the duration of the rotate cycle, you will end up creating more logs. C would just stop creating additional logs, defeating the purpose of log rotation. D doesn't change the fact that logs are being created too frequently, it just puts them somewhere else. So the only viable answer is A - increase the audit.log file size in the appropriate configuration file.",
      "Increasing the file size limit for the audit.log file would delay log rotation until the file reaches the specified size. This can be a valid solution if the issue is that the file is rotating too frequently due to reaching its size limit quickly.",
      "B.\n\nTo decrease the frequency of log rotation for the /var/log/audit/audit.log file without losing any information in the logs, the server administrator should decrease the duration of the log rotate cycle. This can be achieved by adjusting the logrotate configuration for the audit log files to specify a longer time interval or a larger file size threshold before rotation occurs."
    ],
    "url": "https://www.examtopics.com/discussions/comptia/view/135838-exam-sk0-005-topic-1-question-269-discussion/"
  },
  {
    "exam_code": "sk0-005",
    "topic": 1,
    "question_num": 270,
    "question": "Which of the following backup types copies changed data from a server and then combines the backups on the backup target?",
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "Differential",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "Incremental",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "Synthetic full",
        "is_correct": true
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "Snapshot",
        "is_correct": false
      }
    ],
    "suggested_answers": [
      "C"
    ],
    "answers": [
      "C. Synthetic full",
      "C. Synthetic full.\nThis is a type of backup that copies changed data from a server and then combines the backups on the backup target. A synthetic full backup is a backup type that creates a full backup from an existing full backup and subsequent incremental backups. A synthetic full backup does not require accessing the source data again, but only the backup data that is already stored on the backup target. A synthetic full backup offers the benefits of reducing the backup window, network traffic, and storage space while providing faster and easier recovery than incremental backups."
    ],
    "url": "https://www.examtopics.com/discussions/comptia/view/112956-exam-sk0-005-topic-1-question-270-discussion/"
  },
  {
    "exam_code": "sk0-005",
    "topic": 1,
    "question_num": 271,
    "question": "A server administrator is taking advantage of all the available bandwidth of the four NICs on the server. Which of the following NIC-teaming technologies should the server administrator utilize?",
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "Fail over",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "Fault tolerance",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "Load balancing",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "Link aggregation",
        "is_correct": true
      }
    ],
    "suggested_answers": [
      "D"
    ],
    "answers": [
      "he correct answer is: D. Link aggregation\n\n✅ Explanation:\nLink aggregation (also known as NIC teaming or port trunking) combines multiple network interfaces (NICs) into a single logical interface to:\n\nIncrease bandwidth (i.e., use the combined capacity of all NICs)\n\nProvide redundancy in case one NIC fails\n\nSince the goal is to take advantage of all the available bandwidth, link aggregation is the right choice.",
      "Answer is D",
      "Answer is D.",
      "The only option here directly relating to increasing bandwidth is D.",
      "the answer is correct because\nLink aggregation increases bandwidth, provides graceful degradation as failure occurs, increases availability and provides load-balancing capabilities. Load balancing enables the device to divide incoming and outgoing traffic along multiple interfaces to reduce congestion in the network.",
      "C. Load balancing\nLoad balancing NIC-teaming technology allows the server to distribute network traffic across multiple network interfaces (NICs) to take advantage of all available bandwidth. This results in increased network throughput and improved performance. Load balancing distributes incoming network traffic evenly across the active NICs, preventing any single NIC from becoming a bottleneck.",
      "No, Load Balancing and NIC-teaming are two different sections in the CompTIA Server+ student guide. Under the NIC-teaming section Link Aggregation is listed and directly applies to the bandwidth scenario."
    ],
    "url": "https://www.examtopics.com/discussions/comptia/view/135839-exam-sk0-005-topic-1-question-271-discussion/"
  },
  {
    "exam_code": "sk0-005",
    "topic": 1,
    "question_num": 272,
    "question": "A technician is working on a Linux server and is trying to access another server over the network. The technician gets server not found messages when trying to execute ping servername but no error messages when using ping servername.domain.com. Which of the following should the technician do to resolve the error?",
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "Configure the domain search variable.",
        "is_correct": true
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "Change the permissions on resolv.conf.",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "Configure the DNS address.",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "Modify nsswitch.conf.",
        "is_correct": false
      }
    ],
    "suggested_answers": [
      "A"
    ],
    "answers": [
      "navigate to your system's network settings, locate the DNS settings section, and within that section, you will typically find a field labeled \"Domain Search\" or \"Search Domains\" where you can input a list of domain names separated by spaces, which your device will use to attempt to resolve hostnames that are not fully qualified by automatically appending each domain from the list until a successful resolution is found; essentially,"
    ],
    "url": "https://www.examtopics.com/discussions/comptia/view/153198-exam-sk0-005-topic-1-question-272-discussion/"
  },
  {
    "exam_code": "sk0-005",
    "topic": 1,
    "question_num": 273,
    "question": "Which of the following licensing models is MOST appropriate for a data center that has a variable daily equipment count?",
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "Per site",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "Per server",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "Per user",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "Per core",
        "is_correct": true
      }
    ],
    "suggested_answers": [
      "D"
    ],
    "answers": [
      "Most \"appropriate\" would be \"A. per site\", adjusting costs for server and core as they vary daily would be a nightmare.",
      "It should be A. so you don't constantly change your licensing as the equipment changes within a site.",
      "Chat GPT says its per-server. The explanation is as follows\nLicensing models based on server count are designed to accommodate fluctuating hardware configurations, making them suitable for environments where the number of servers can change regularly. This model allows you to license the software based on the number of physical servers or virtual machines (VMs) that are deployed or actively running at any given time.\n\nBy using a server-based licensing model, you can easily scale your licensing needs up or down to match the changing equipment count in your data center. This flexibility ensures that you are compliant with licensing requirements while efficiently managing the licensing costs.",
      "Well Microsoft Bing says is per-server while Bard says is per-core"
    ],
    "url": "https://www.examtopics.com/discussions/comptia/view/111108-exam-sk0-005-topic-1-question-273-discussion/"
  },
  {
    "exam_code": "sk0-005",
    "topic": 1,
    "question_num": 274,
    "question": "While running a local network security scan, an administrator discovers communication between clients and one of the web servers is happening in cleartext. Company policy requires all communication to be encrypted. Which of the following ports should be closed to stop the cleartext communication?",
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "21",
        "is_correct": true
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "22",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "443",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "3389",
        "is_correct": false
      }
    ],
    "suggested_answers": [
      "A"
    ],
    "answers": [
      "21 = FTP *cleartext; no need to go further",
      "To stop cleartext communication with a web server, you would want to close port 80, which is the standard port for HTTP (cleartext web traffic). However, port 80 is not listed in your options.\n\nFrom the options given:\n\nA. 21 - FTP (File Transfer Protocol, typically cleartext)\nB. 22 - SSH (Secure Shell, encrypted)\nC. 443 - HTTPS (HTTP Secure, encrypted)\nD. 3389 - RDP (Remote Desktop Protocol)\n\nThe correct answer to stop cleartext communication would be:\n\nA. 21 (FTP)\n\nHowever, since the question specifically mentioned web servers, it's possible there might be a mistake in the options provided, as port 80 (HTTP) would be the most appropriate choice for web server cleartext communication."
    ],
    "url": "https://www.examtopics.com/discussions/comptia/view/123992-exam-sk0-005-topic-1-question-274-discussion/"
  },
