  {
    "exam_code": "sk0-005",
    "topic": 1,
    "question_num": 277,
    "question": "A systems administrator is attempting to install a package on a server. After downloading the package from the internet and trying to launch it, the installation is blocked by the antivirus on the server. Which of the following must be completed before launching the installation package again?",
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "Creating an exclusion to the antivirus for the application",
        "is_correct": true
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "Disabling real-time scanning by the antivirus",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "Validating the checksum for the downloaded installation package",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "Checking for corruption of the downloaded installation package",
        "is_correct": false
      }
    ],
    "suggested_answers": [
      "A"
    ],
    "answers": [
      "The verbage states \"Which of the following MUST be completed before launching the installation package again?\". You do not HAVE to validate the checksum before launching the installation package again, although this is best practice, but you do HAVE to create an exclusion or the AV will still block installation. Answer is A.",
      "The didn't say it was corrupt, they specifically say AV is blocking it. So an AV exclusion is needed, not checksum.",
      "C. Validating the checksum for the downloaded installation package",
      "When downloading packages from the internet, especially for servers, it's crucial to ensure the integrity and authenticity of the package. This helps to ensure that the package hasn't been tampered with and that it's safe to install. The most common way to do this is by checking the checksum of the downloaded file against a known good checksum provided by the software vendor.\n\nTherefore, the best practice in this scenario is:\n\nC. Validating the checksum for the downloaded installation package.\n\nBUT, this question seems to assume the file has Integrity already"
    ],
    "url": "https://www.examtopics.com/discussions/comptia/view/123994-exam-sk0-005-topic-1-question-277-discussion/"
  },
  {
    "exam_code": "sk0-005",
    "topic": 1,
    "question_num": 278,
    "question": "An administrator needs to increase the size of an existing RAID 6 array that is running out of available space. Which of the following is the BEST way the administrator can perform this task?",
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "Replace all the array drives at once and then expand the array.",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "Expand the array by changing the RAID level to 6.",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "Expand the array by changing the RAID level to 10.",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "Replace the array drives one at a time and then expand the array.",
        "is_correct": true
      }
    ],
    "suggested_answers": [
      "D"
    ],
    "answers": [
      "By replacing the drives one at a time and allowing the array to rebuild after each replacement, you can gradually increase the size of the array without losing data. Once all drives have been replaced, you can then expand the array to make use of the additional space. This approach minimizes the risk of data loss compared to replacing all drives at once.",
      "I am curious, don't the hard drives in the RAID have to be the same size (and ideally the same make) before we can build an array? If we have to replace one at a time, won't that mess the RAID configuration up?",
      "Yes to first question but obv not possible here, and no to 2nd question. If you replace, and wait for array to rebuild, you won't lose your data."
    ],
    "url": "https://www.examtopics.com/discussions/comptia/view/120878-exam-sk0-005-topic-1-question-278-discussion/"
  },
  {
    "exam_code": "sk0-005",
    "topic": 1,
    "question_num": 279,
    "question": "A systems administrator recently upgraded the memory in a server, and now the server does not turn on, and nothing is displayed on the screen. Which of the following is the NEXT step the administrator should take to diagnose the error without opening the machine?",
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "Perform a cold reboot.",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "Listen for POST code beeps.",
        "is_correct": true
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "Call technical support.",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "Check the monitor connection.",
        "is_correct": false
      }
    ],
    "suggested_answers": [
      "B"
    ],
    "answers": [
      "A beep code is the audio signal from a computer when it first powers on to give the Power-On Self-Test (POST) result. Typically, one short beep means that it passed the test successfully or a series of beeps means that something is wrong with the computer hardware."
    ],
    "url": "https://www.examtopics.com/discussions/comptia/view/153200-exam-sk0-005-topic-1-question-279-discussion/"
  },
  {
