        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "P2V",
        "is_correct": true
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "VM cloning",
        "is_correct": false
      }
    ],
    "suggested_answers": [
      "C"
    ],
    "answers": [
      "P2V stands for \"physical to virtual\" and is a process that moves a physical computer's operating system, applications, and data to a virtual machine (VM):"
    ],
    "url": "https://www.examtopics.com/discussions/comptia/view/153202-exam-sk0-005-topic-1-question-282-discussion/"
  },
  {
    "exam_code": "sk0-005",
    "topic": 1,
    "question_num": 283,
    "question": "An administrator is troubleshooting an application performance issue on a virtual server with two vCPUs. The application performance logs indicate CPU contention. The administrator adds more vCPU cores to the VM, yet the issue persists. Which of the following is the MOST likely reason for this issue?",
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "The server has high page utilization.",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "The server has high disk latency.",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "The application is single-threaded.",
        "is_correct": true
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "The application cannot be virtualized.",
        "is_correct": false
      }
    ],
    "suggested_answers": [
      "C"
    ],
    "answers": [
      "C. The application is single-threaded.\n\nAdding more vCPU cores to a virtual machine may not necessarily improve performance if the application is single-threaded. In a single-threaded application, it can only effectively utilize one CPU core at a time. Even if more vCPUs are added, the application won't be able to take advantage of the additional cores, leading to continued CPU contention."
    ],
    "url": "https://www.examtopics.com/discussions/comptia/view/128600-exam-sk0-005-topic-1-question-283-discussion/"
  },
  {
    "exam_code": "sk0-005",
    "topic": 1,
    "question_num": 284,
    "question": "An administrator is alerted to an issue with server hardware deployed as a virtualization host. The alert indicates a predictive failure in a DIMM module. Which of the following is the BEST action for the administrator to take?",
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "Replace the failed module during the next scheduled downtime.",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "Hot swap the failed module immediately.",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "Schedule an outage to replace the failed module immediately.",
        "is_correct": true
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "Hot swap the failed module during the next scheduled downtime.",
        "is_correct": false
      }
    ],
    "suggested_answers": [
      "C"
    ],
    "answers": [
      "Hot-swapping memory, while possible in some advanced server systems, is not a standard feature across all hardware. It's risky to assume this capability exists without specific knowledge of the system. By scheduling an immediate outage, the administrator is taking a proactive stance to prevent potential data loss or system instability.",
      "Scheduling an outage immediately mitigates the possibility of a catastrophic failure, thus eliminating the inevitable unannounced disruption of services.",
      "B. Hot swap the failed module immediately.\n\nDIMM modules are normally designed to be hot-swappable.\nIf a DIMM module is expected to fail, it is important to replace the failed module as soon as possible. Hot-swapping (in-service replacement) will minimize service interruptions.",
      "Here's why:\n\nIt addresses the urgency of the predictive failure alert, reducing the risk of an actual failure occurring.\nScheduling an outage allows for proper planning and notification to affected users or systems.\nIt ensures the replacement can be done safely, with the system powered down, which is typically necessary for memory replacement.\nThis approach balances the need for prompt action with the need for controlled, safe maintenance on a critical system.\nIt allows for proper testing after replacement to ensure system stability.\n\nWhile scheduling an immediate outage may cause some short-term disruption, it's the safest and most responsible course of action given the critical nature of a virtualization host and the potential consequences of an actual memory failure. This approach minimizes the risk of unplanned downtime or data loss that could result from an actual failure of the DIMM module.",
      "DIMM's can not be hot swappable",
      "B or A?",
      "A, because DIMM's can not be hot swappable."
    ],
    "url": "https://www.examtopics.com/discussions/comptia/view/118669-exam-sk0-005-topic-1-question-284-discussion/"
  },
  {
    "exam_code": "sk0-005",
    "topic": 1,
    "question_num": 285,
    "question": "A technician is troubleshooting a server issue. The technician has determined several possible causes of the issue and has identified various solutions. Which of the following should the technician do NEXT?",
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "Consult internet forums to determine which is the most common cause and deploy only that solution.",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "Test each solution individually to determine the root cause, rolling back the changes in between each test.",
        "is_correct": true
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "Implement the shortest solution first to identify the issue and minimize downtime.",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "Test each solution in succession and restore the server from the latest snapshot.",
        "is_correct": false
      }
    ],
    "suggested_answers": [
      "B"
    ],
    "answers": [
      "B. Test each solution individually to determine the root cause, rolling back the changes in between each test."
    ],
    "url": "https://www.examtopics.com/discussions/comptia/view/128601-exam-sk0-005-topic-1-question-285-discussion/"
  },
  {
    "exam_code": "sk0-005",
    "topic": 1,
    "question_num": 286,
    "question": "Following a recent power outage, a server in the data center has been constantly going offline and losing its configuration. Users have been experiencing access issues while using the application on the server. The server technician notices the date and time are incorrect when the server is online. All other servers are working. Which of the following would MOST likely cause this issue? (Choose two.)",
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "The server has a faulty power supply.",
        "is_correct": true
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "The server has a CMOS battery failure.",
        "is_correct": true
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "The server requires OS updates.",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "The server has a malfunctioning LED panel.",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "E",
        "text": "The servers have NTP configured.",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "F",
        "text": "CPU frequency scaling is set too high.",
        "is_correct": false
      }
    ],
    "suggested_answers": [
      "A",
      "B"
    ],
    "answers": [
      ". The server has a faulty power supply:\n\nA faulty power supply can lead to unexpected shutdowns or power interruptions, causing the server to go offline. These abrupt shutdowns may result in the loss of configuration settings, including date and time.\nB. The server has a CMOS battery failure:\n\nThe CMOS battery is responsible for maintaining the system's date and time settings when the server is powered off. If the CMOS battery is failing, it may not be able to retain the date and time information, leading to incorrect settings each time the server is restarted."
    ],
    "url": "https://www.examtopics.com/discussions/comptia/view/128729-exam-sk0-005-topic-1-question-286-discussion/"
  },
