  {
    "exam_code": "sk0-005",
    "topic": 1,
    "question_num": 338,
    "question": "An engineer is configuring the RAID on a new server that will support a large database handling real-time data processing. The system needs to have the largest available space with the best write performance. Given the following disks and configurations:\n\n• Four 300GB SSD disks\n• Eight 1.2TB 15K SAS disks\n• Must include redundant OS disk\n\nWhich of the following configurations would meet all the necessary requirements for the new database server?",
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "OS-Two SSD RAID 1 -\n\nDB logs - Two SSD RAID 1 -\n\nData - Six 1.2TB 15K SAS RAID 5 -\nTWO hot-spare disks 1.2TB 15K SAS",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "OS - One SSD RAID 0 -\n\nDB logs - One SSD RAID 0 -\nData - Eight 1.2TB 15K SAS RAID 6\nTwo hot-spare disks SSD Disks",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "OS - Two SSD RAID 1 -\n\nDB logs - Two SSD RAID 1 -\nData - Eight 1.2TB 15K SAS RAID 5\nNo hot-spare disks",
        "is_correct": true
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "OS - Two SSD RAID 0 -\n\nDB Logs - Two SSD RAID 1 -\nData - Eight 1.2TB 15K SAS RAID 5\nNo hot-spare disks",
        "is_correct": false
      }
    ],
    "suggested_answers": [
      "C"
    ],
    "answers": [
      "Going with (C)\n* Only reason it isn't A is because question asked for \"the most available space\" which C delivers.",
      "Why the Other Options Are Incorrect:\nB. OS - One SSD RAID 0, DB Logs - One SSD RAID 0, Data - Eight 1.2TB 15K SAS RAID 6:\n\nRAID 0 offers no redundancy for the OS or DB logs, which violates the requirement for redundant OS disks.\nRAID 6 provides high redundancy (tolerates two disk failures), but it sacrifices write performance, which is not ideal for real-time data processing.\nC. OS - Two SSD RAID 1, DB Logs - Two SSD RAID 1, Data - Eight 1.2TB 15K SAS RAID 5, No hot-spare disks:\n\nWhile this setup meets most requirements, the absence of hot spares leaves the system vulnerable during rebuilds after a drive failure, reducing overall reliability.\nD. OS - Two SSD RAID 0, DB Logs - Two SSD RAID 1, Data - Eight 1.2TB 15K SAS RAID 5, No hot-spare disks:\n\nRAID 0 for the OS provides no redundancy, which is unacceptable for critical systems. Additionally, the absence of hot spares weakens fault tolerance.",
      "C. Personal feelings aside (and mentioned later) it's the most capacity and provides OS redundancy.\n\nI absolutely abhor this answer, because the big thing with RAID 5 is hot swappable redundant disks, and not having any spare disks is borderline painful, but having all 8 disks active means you have more storage than having 6 of the disks active and 2 spares. In an ideal world, C would be 7 active and 1 spare drive, but that's not what it is. Given RAID 5 loses the equivalent of 1 complete disk to parity information, the difference is 7TB with 2.4 sitting on the shelf, or 9.4TB with nothing sitting on the shelf for when a drive fails. It goes against every best practice I have ever run up against, but C is technically the most correct answer, even if it's not \"best practice.\""
    ],
    "url": "https://www.examtopics.com/discussions/comptia/view/149423-exam-sk0-005-topic-1-question-338-discussion/"
  },
  {
    "exam_code": "sk0-005",
    "topic": 1,
    "question_num": 339,
    "question": "An analyst is planning a new point-to-point Ethernet connection between a university campus and a newly acquired space downtown that is about 5mi (8km) away. The analyst would like the fastest possible connection speed. Which of the following would best meet the analyst’s needs?",
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "1000BASE-LX 1Gb single-mode plenum fiber connection",
        "is_correct": true
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "10GBASE-T 10Gb copper plenum Ethernet connection",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "1000BASE-T 1Gb copper non-plenum Ethernet connection",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "10GBASE-SR 10Gb multimode plenum fiber connection",
        "is_correct": false
      }
    ],
    "suggested_answers": [
      "A"
    ],
    "answers": [
      "For a 5‑mile (8km) point‑to‑point Ethernet connection with the fastest possible speed, the best choice is:\n👉 Single‑mode fiber (SMF) with long‑range optics (1000BASE‑LX or 10GBASE‑LR)."
    ],
    "url": "https://www.examtopics.com/discussions/comptia/view/339326-exam-sk0-005-topic-1-question-339-discussion/"
  },
  {
    "exam_code": "sk0-005",
    "topic": 1,
    "question_num": 340,
    "question": "A technician is attempting to resolve an issue with a file server that is unable to download a file. Given the following output:\n\n\n\nWhich of the following would best allow this file to be read?",
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "chown",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "sestatus",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "setenforce",
        "is_correct": true
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "getenforce",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "E",
        "text": "chmod",
        "is_correct": false
      }
    ],
    "suggested_answers": [
      "C"
    ],
    "answers": [
      "Since owner has read/write and everyone else has read-only a chmod wouldn't help or be necessary here, a setenforce (C) would allow you to swap between enforcing/permissive modes to see if SELinux policies are preventing read.",
      "The output seems to indicate that the OS is protected with SELinux and probably sudo setenforce 0 can be used to put it in permissive mode"
    ],
    "url": "https://www.examtopics.com/discussions/comptia/view/135088-exam-sk0-005-topic-1-question-340-discussion/"
  },
  {
    "exam_code": "sk0-005",
