  },
  {
    "exam_code": "sk0-005",
    "topic": 1,
    "question_num": 370,
    "question": "Which of the following attacks is the most difficult to mitigate with technology?",
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "Ransomware",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "Backdoor",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "SQL injection",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "Phishing",
        "is_correct": true
      }
    ],
    "suggested_answers": [
      "D"
    ],
    "answers": [
      "A. Ransomware (INCORRECT)\nRansomware can be mitigated with advanced endpoint protection, regular backups, and user education. Technologies like anti-malware tools, intrusion detection systems, and regular software updates are effective against ransomware.\n\nB. Backdoor (INCORRECT)\nBackdoors can be mitigated through regular system and network monitoring, vulnerability assessments, and the use of security tools designed to detect unauthorized access points.\n\nC. SQL Injection (INCORRECT)\nSQL injection can be mitigated with proper coding practices, such as the use of prepared statements and input validation. Web application firewalls (WAFs) and code audits are solutions against SQL injection attacks.\n\n***D. Phishing (CORRECT)\nPhishing primarily exploits human factors rather than technology vulnerabilities, making it challenging to mitigate purely with technology.",
      "D. Phishing\n\nPhishing attacks rely heavily on social engineering tactics, manipulating individuals into divulging sensitive information or performing actions that compromise security. While technological measures such as email filters and anti-phishing tools can help detect and mitigate phishing attempts to some extent, they cannot entirely eliminate the human factor involved. Education and awareness among users are crucial to effectively combat phishing attacks.",
      "Mitigating each of these attacks presents its own set of challenges, but generally speaking, SQL injection (option C) is often considered one of the most difficult to mitigate with technology alone.\n\nSQL injection attacks target the vulnerability in web applications that use SQL databases. By injecting malicious SQL code into input fields, attackers can manipulate the database queries executed by the application, potentially gaining unauthorized access to data or performing other malicious actions."
    ],
    "url": "https://www.examtopics.com/discussions/comptia/view/135180-exam-sk0-005-topic-1-question-370-discussion/"
  },
  {
    "exam_code": "sk0-005",
    "topic": 1,
    "question_num": 371,
    "question": "A systems administrator needs to create a data volume out of four disks with the most redundancy. Which of the following is the best solution?",
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "RAID 0",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "RAID 1",
        "is_correct": true
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "RAID 5",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "RAID 6",
        "is_correct": false
      }
    ],
    "suggested_answers": [
      "B"
    ],
    "answers": [
      "The correct answer is D. RAID 6.\n🔹 Why?\nRAID 6 offers high redundancy by using two parity blocks.\nIt can tolerate up to two disk failures without losing data.\nSince the administrator wants the most redundancy, RAID 6 is better than RAID 5 (which can only survive one disk failure).\n\n🔹 Quick overview of the other options:\nRAID 0: No redundancy (just speed).\nRAID 1: Mirrors disks, but with four disks, you would only get the capacity of two disks.\nRAID 5: Some redundancy (can survive one disk failure), but less than RAID 6.",
      "Explanation:\nRAID 6 provides the most redundancy while using four disks by implementing dual parity. This allows the system to tolerate up to two simultaneous drive failures without data loss, which is the highest level of redundancy among the options provided.",
      "B.\nThe most redundancy would be raid 1. This would mirror every drive in the raid allowing up to 3 drives to fail. Raid 6 allows 2 drives to fail. So the “best” redundancy would be raid 1 even though in a real server an admin would more likely choose raid 5/6 to have more space then only a single drive."
    ],
    "url": "https://www.examtopics.com/discussions/comptia/view/138922-exam-sk0-005-topic-1-question-371-discussion/"
  },
  {
    "exam_code": "sk0-005",
    "topic": 1,
    "question_num": 372,
    "question": "After a technician upgrades the firmware on a database server that is connected to two external storage arrays, the server prompts the technician to configure RAID. The technician knows the server had several configured RAID sets and thinks the firmware upgrade cleared the RAID configurations. Which of the following should the technician do to troubleshoot this issue?",
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "Power cycle the storage arrays and rescan RAID on the server.",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "Boot the OS into recovery mode and rescan the disks.",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "Restore the default RAID configuration and reboot.",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "Perform a rescan on the server's RAID controller.",
        "is_correct": true
      }
    ],
    "suggested_answers": [
      "D"
    ],
    "answers": [
      "D - Given answer is correct.",
      "A. Power Cycle and Rescan:\nShut down the server completely.\nDisconnect the power cords from the server and storage arrays.\nWait a few minutes, then reconnect the power cords and power up the server.\nDuring startup, enter the RAID controller's setup utility (usually by pressing a key like Ctrl+I or F2 during boot) and attempt to rescan the RAID arrays.",
      "D - Just go for the rescan, disconnecting power cords does nothing."
    ],
    "url": "https://www.examtopics.com/discussions/comptia/view/302798-exam-sk0-005-topic-1-question-372-discussion/"
  },
  {
    "exam_code": "sk0-005",
    "topic": 1,
    "question_num": 373,
    "question": "A server administrator recently installed a kernel update to test functionality. Upon reboot, the administrator determined the new kernel was not compatible with certain server hardware and was unable to uninstall the update. Which of the following should the administrator do to mitigate further issues with the newly installed kernel version?",
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "Edit the bootloader configuration file and change the first kernel stanza to reflect the file location for the last known-good kernel files.",
        "is_correct": true
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "Perform a complete OS reinstall on the server using the same media that was used during the initial install.",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "Edit the bootloader configuration file and move the newest kernel update stanza to the end of the file.",
        "is_correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "Set a BIOS password to prevent server technicians from making any changes to the system.",
        "is_correct": false
      }
    ],
    "suggested_answers": [
      "A"
    ],
    "answers": [
      "A. Edit the bootloader configuration file and change the first kernel stanza to reflect the file location for the last known-good kernel files.\n\nThis approach allows the server to boot using a previous, stable version of the kernel that is known to be compatible with the server's hardware. By editing the bootloader configuration, the administrator can ensure that the system bypasses the problematic kernel version during the boot process, effectively mitigating the immediate issues caused by the incompatible kernel update. This solution is preferable because it directly addresses the problem without the need for more drastic measures such as a complete OS reinstall or adjusting unrelated security settings like BIOS passwords. Moreover, it preserves the server's configuration and data, avoiding the downtime and work associated with reinstallation."
    ],
    "url": "https://www.examtopics.com/discussions/comptia/view/135966-exam-sk0-005-topic-1-question-373-discussion/"
  },
  {
    "exam_code": "sk0-005",
    "topic": 1,
    "question_num": 374,
    "question": "The accounting department needs more storage and wants to retain the current data for quick read-write access. The accounting server does not have any internal drive bays available to keep both disks; however, the server does have USB 3.0 and eSATA ports available. Which of the following is the best way to accomplish the department's goals?",
    "options": [
